window.harnessChunks["rows-e657733bc4480fff.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:48:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LLaMAR</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR/tree/d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\">d2b74ca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute the embodied work required to complete the shared task through robot-specific actions in the environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial observations, object locations/states, navigation/manipulation requirements, action failures and changing environment state during a long-horizon episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each robot&#39;s next substantive environment action from the current observations/state and interpret returned success/failure when selecting what happens next. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven LLaMAR action/controller path over the embodied robot streams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AI2-THOR/SAR environment adapters, feasible-action mapping, per-agent observations/states/action histories, task/subtask state and deterministic execution plumbing. - Closure path: team task/current plan -&gt; model selects an action for each robot -&gt; environment executes each embodied action -&gt; per-agent observation/state/success/failure returns -&gt; later model call selects changed robot actions -&gt; episode progresses. - Boundary reachability: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py and SAR/baselines/llamar.py are shipped runnable first-party episode entrypoints and directly execute the credited action/environment feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: simulator code transports actions and state, while the substantive next-action choices are model-generated from the live episode context. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: next-action authority is centralized in the model call rather than separately modelled inside each physical robot, but the operational behavior is still first-party agent-owned at the assessed team boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among simultaneously operating embodied robots sharing the same physical workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robots blocking/bumping one another, congestion around shared objects/receptacles, one robot&#39;s manipulation/navigation preventing another robot from completing its assigned action, and unnecessary simultaneous movement into the same constrained area. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose joint next-step actions such as waiting, moving aside, closing an obstruction or delaying one robot so another robot can proceed without cross-agent interference. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven joint action/controller path using whole-team state and failure context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared observations/state, previous action/failure fields, combined task/memory state, explicit anti-blocking prompt constraints and per-robot joint action output. - Closure path: multiple robot operations interact in one room -&gt; execution exposes cross-agent blocking/failure -&gt; the next model call reasons about the interference -&gt; assigns compensating actions across the involved robots -&gt; later robot behavior changes and the blocked operation can proceed. - Boundary reachability: the standard LLaMAR actor prompt is called on every episode step from the runnable baseline and directly receives/returns the team state and per-agent joint actions used by env.step(). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference choice is contextual model reasoning over current cross-agent state; deterministic code only executes the selected joint actions. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic task allocation is not the basis for S2; the positive state rests on the separately evidenced physical interference problem and feedback relation. - Distinct S1 units: the concurrently acting embodied robot streams (Alice, Bob and additional configured robots), each with its own observation/state/action/success history and physical action effect. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one robot can physically block/bump another or occupy/open an object/path such that another robot&#39;s navigation/manipulation fails or must wait. - Attenuating coordination relation: the joint controller sees all robots&#39; state/history and is explicitly instructed to avoid blocking/bumping; failure reasoning can prescribe wait/move-aside/clear-path behavior across the involved robots. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the model&#39;s coordinated per-robot action dictionary is passed through actionchecker and then to env.step(), changing the next embodied action of each robot. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the decisive witness is regulation of a concrete interaction disturbance among simultaneous embodied operations, not the existence of multiple robots, shared memory or a task decomposition graph.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain the whole team&#39;s current task commitments and intervene in the active multi-robot plan as completion/failure/environment information changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing team progress, completed versus still-open commitments, newly observed environment information and failures requiring the active plan or current robot allocation/actions to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the current shared open-subtask plan for the team and, through the whole-team action/controller stage, determine which operational action each robot should take now under that plan. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven planner and whole-team action/controller path. - Whole-system current view: the planner receives the task, current observation from every configured robot, and the shared open/completed subtask state after each verifier step; the action/controller additionally receives every robot&#39;s state, previous action/failure, shared subtasks and combined memory. - Current-control decision scope: preserve or revise the team&#39;s active unfinished commitments and assign the next concrete actions/subtasks across the current robot population, including interventions after failures and current spatial conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared open/completed-subtask state, verifier updates, whole-team observation aggregation, combined memory, planner prompt, action/controller prompt and repeated plan-update loop. - Closure path: robot operations execute -&gt; team observations/actions and completion state update -&gt; verifier updates completed commitments -&gt; planner forms a revised whole-team open plan -&gt; action/controller maps that current plan/state to robot-specific next actions -&gt; changed operations execute and report back. - Boundary reachability: the planner/action stages are invoked in every standard runnable LLaMAR episode; no external orchestrator is required to form the whole-team view or return interventions into robot operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic choice of the current plan and per-robot intervention is model-driven; deterministic code stores plan/completion state and transports decisions into the environment. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 credit is not based on the component name Planner; it rests on the whole-team current view plus returned decisions changing the active shared commitments and robot actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: the verifier provides in-band completion reporting, but no materially independent complementary-audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect assumptions about whether a subtask has completed can be corrected before replanning, but the checked claim remains part of the ordinary task-progress loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor with a distinct authority/evidence channel is established; the verifier prompt only classifies completed subtasks for the same episode. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3; the verifier uses the ordinary model service/configuration and shared episode evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt, all robot observations/states/previous actions, shared open/completed subtasks and combined memory. - Closure path: robot actions execute -&gt; ordinary episode observations feed verifier -&gt; completed-subtask list feeds planner -&gt; current plan changes; this is ordinary progress reporting/monitoring without an evidenced independent audit boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a semantic verifier exists, but verifier naming and a separate prompt-stage do not establish complementary independence. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verifier does influence subsequent planning; the missing requirement is organizational/complementary independence, not lack of feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective capability-adaptation loop is established beyond current-episode planning/replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: LLaMAR adapts its current task plan to new observations and failures, but does not evidence an actor that senses an external future environment, generates capability-change options and installs a change for later operational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective adaptation of the installed harness/robot organization across episodes. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current observation updates, planner replanning, memory and failure reasoning. - Closure path: environment change/failure -&gt; current episode replanning -&gt; changed immediate robot actions; no future-oriented capability option is implemented into later installed capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current replanning is S1/S3 regulation, not evidence of S4 merely because the plan changes. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: long-horizon planning and replanning are substantial capabilities but remain inside the present episode boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy authority is established at the multi-robot episode boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task text, model/configuration parameters, prompt rules and simulator limits constrain behavior but do not constitute identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining or reconciling the team&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy under competing S3/S4 demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-selected task/scene/configuration, static prompts and runtime constraints. - Closure path: external task/configuration enters directly as operating constraints; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models control current operational/coordination/management choices but do not choose the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. - Evidence: README.md; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static system prompts describing robot roles/constraints are not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563/assessments/llamar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563\">a6735e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:28:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgenticX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX/tree/582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\">582c9d0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended user tasks by choosing tools, using execution results, and modifying task artifacts through root/child provider-backed agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task details, tool errors, repository content, provider responses and subtask outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next model tool call, interpret feedback and continue/revise an execution plan. - Decision owner: configured model-backed AgentRuntime/ReAct root and spawned agents; neither the SDK Agent data model nor the event queue owns model reasoning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider resolver, agent/tool loop, Studio session persistence, subagent context/tool permissions and callbacks. - Closure path: model chooses a tool, runtime executes it, the result enters the same agent&#39;s context, and later decisions change task output. - Boundary reachability: this path is shipped both through the agx/Studio local backend and embedded SDK; Near&#39;s default app starts this local runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent model makes contextual tool/continuation decisions; host code supplies guardrails and transport. - Evidence: AgentRuntime, ReActAgent, Studio server Meta-Agent setup. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: successful live provider/model execution and claimed production quality were not measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidence-backed distinct relation that autonomously attenuates an identified interference among simultaneously acting S1 units, beyond task partition and bounded queueing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential overlap of parallel workers, resource contention and conflicting changes would require interference-specific handling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned cross-worker conflict detection, reconciliation or isolation decision shown as an operationally closed coordination loop for the declared Studio team. - Decision owner: none established at S2; the model planner assigns tasks and host TaskLock transports actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team task decomposition, worker assignments/dependencies, TaskLock action queue, group routing and shared context. - Closure path: delegations can complete and summaries return, but that is ordinary task routing; no distinct detected inter-S1 collision and corrective relation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming a CoordinatorAgent or serializing actions does not establish function-specific S2 conflict attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model-backed child agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file edits or contested resources are plausible, but the inspected supported team path does not independently establish a response to an observed conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: no qualifying inter-S1 relation established; task lists and a queue prevent generic scheduling races, not semantically competing work ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: worker results and messages return to the manager/leader, but without a verified conflict-specific control decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2-specific ownership is not credited for generic dispatch or action queue operations. - Evidence: Team mode wiring and TaskLock, TaskLock, WorkforcePattern. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: developers may compose custom S2 adapters with the SDK; that is not automatically the shipped Studio default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root-session team by observing children and deciding when to spawn, communicate, retry or cancel during live task delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active child runs may stall/fail, saturate concurrency or CPU/memory, need new work or require intervention to meet the immediate user task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and invoke status query, spawn/cancel/retry/message subagent tools in light of reported current child state and goals. - Decision owner: configured root model-backed Meta-Agent within the supported Studio session, not the AgentTeamManager or the deterministic ResourceMonitor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager actual live status registry, timeouts, caps and resource admission checks; root Meta-Agent tool dispatcher; Studio event stream. - Closure path: root sees team status/tool results, chooses a current-team intervention, AgentTeamManager changes a child run/permission to launch, and updated state returns to the root&#39;s next model turn. - Boundary reachability: Studio builds the root Meta-Agent with visible team-management tools, and the attached AgentTeamManager executes them on real session children in the shipped local backend. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current worker-team decisions are controlled by the root model, while deterministic caps merely reject inadmissible actions. - Whole-system current view: querysubagentstatus and team status provide running/completed/failed child state and resource-monitor context for the session team. - Current-control decision scope: the current portfolio of active/retryable child agents in one root session, including admission and cessation; not Enterprise gateway quotas or an unrelated Studio user. - Evidence: team lifecycle/status/cancel, meta tool handler, resource monitor. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model might choose not to use these tools; the claim is an available integrated operational loop, not a guaranteed policy of continuous proactive optimization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently controlled complementary reality-audit path with corrective control established for the current Studio session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor can mistakenly report complete work while the real environment differs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor&#39;s direct-evidence veto/intervention against the team report established in the selected operating mode. - Decision owner: none established; SDK evaluation/LLM judge and Enterprise audit logging are separate or routine surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, tool results, observability and optional evaluation framework. - Closure path: tests/evaluations can be explicitly launched, but a first-party independently owned standing complementary team audit with returned corrective control is not demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation APIs, group worker results and log chains cannot substitute for a separate auditor owner. - Evidence: evaluation/observability overview, team results, Enterprise gateway separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: externally composed independent reviewers are possible through the SDK, but their independence cannot be credited to the shipped session team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: convert observations of changing external tool/provider/task conditions and user corrections into future reusable skill capability for subsequent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external tool failures, newly learned environment conventions and multi-step strategies that existing installed skills do not yet encode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the background model-backed review agent decides whether and how to create/patch a reusable skill; under the normal supervised mode the Desktop operator separately decides whether to approve the proposed future capability. - Decision owner: opt-in skill-review agent in the direct-authorized mode (A), and legitimate Desktop-user approver for a separately supported queued-proposal mode (P). These modes are not simultaneous autonomous governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered SessionReviewHook, actual tool observation records, signal/complexity gate, configured review model, skillmanage, skill policy scan, pending proposals and desktop approval endpoint. - Closure path: a completed tool-using session triggers a review of observed attempts/errors/user corrections; the model proposes a skill; in explicitly authorized direct mode it writes the skill for later sessions, while in default queue mode the Desktop user approves the same proposed document and installs it. Later model runs can discover/use the changed skill collection. - External distinction: observations concern user corrections and tool/provider outcomes from the task environment, not just the host runtime&#39;s own counters; the model interprets their reusable implications. - Future / prospective distinction: a post-session reviewer encodes a method for later task contexts rather than repairing only the completed current task, and the installed skill survives that session boundary. - Adaptation option generated: choose a new action-oriented skill or a patch to an existing skill, including the specific steps and guardrails for future comparable disturbances. - Path back into current capability / S3: after authorized direct installation or Desktop-user approval, the skill is added to the local installed registry so a later Meta-Agent/runtime session can discover and use the revised capability under its current task controls. - Boundary reachability: AgentRuntime registers SessionReviewHook; learning.reviewenabled is an active shipped configuration option, agentwritesrequireapproval distinguishes queue versus direct install, and Studio/Near expose proposal inspection/approve/reject routes. Both paths require configured model/provider access; neither is default-on just because observation is enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model selects substantive future reusable methods, not just a deterministic score; the operator retains the separate approval right in the supervised mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed ultimate-purpose or identity-policy governance path for the local Studio agent-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts about the team&#39;s permanent charter, legitimacy or non-negotiable identity beyond ordinary tasks/permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime identity-level final authority and returned policy choice established in the selected local session hierarchy. - Decision owner: none established for this function; product maintainers, Enterprise administrators and local user are adjacent authorities for different matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator permissions, skill-approval gate, safety policies, user prompts, Enterprise Gateway policies and contributor governance. - Closure path: users permit/deny operations, administrators configure Gateway routing, and skill approvals modify future capabilities; these are operational/safety/adaptation choices rather than a reconstructed identity-level decision loop in local Studio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a Meta-Agent CEO label does not create sovereign identity control, and separate enterprise governance does not transfer to deployed Studio S5. - Evidence: runtime/Enterprise boundaries, Studio tool gates, Meta-Agent identity prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no finding about how the open-source project or enterprise tenants should normatively govern themselves is made.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb/assessments/ag/agenticx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb\">d83ab15 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T17:45:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipecat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat/tree/dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\">dbdf21a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform an environment-facing conversational/agent task by selecting model responses and model-callable tools from live input/context and incorporating tool/service outcomes into subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user utterances, conversation state, tool needs/results, external-service responses, media events and task-specific runtime outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next response/tool action in the shipped model/tool loop. - Decision owner: the configured LLM actor invoked through Pipecat&#39;s first-party LLM service/pipeline surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context aggregators; tool schemas/callbacks; transports; pipeline frames; jobs; timeouts; service adapters; tracing/metrics. - Closure path: user/environment input enters pipeline → Pipecat constructs model context/tools → model chooses response/tool action → runtime executes/delivers it → observations/results are returned into later context and operation. - Boundary reachability: this is Pipecat&#39;s ordinary supported runtime, not a repository-development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model decision actor leaves transport/execution machinery but removes the open-ended choice of conversational/task action. - Evidence: README.md; llmworker.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider implementation is external, but Pipecat deliberately places the model actor inside its supported operational feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concurrent/interleaved output interference between distinct autonomous LLM workers during a live handoff and ensure that only the selected worker owns the next conversational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, causing both workers&#39; outputs to arrive interleaved; overlapping activation would make ownership of the user-facing turn ambiguous. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when to transfer conversational ownership from the current autonomous worker to another worker in response to the live conversation. - Decision owner: the currently active LLM worker in the first-party local-handoff mode, through the model-callable transfertoagent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activateworker; source-pipeline drain; mutually exclusive activation/deactivation; shared worker registry/bus; tool result that suppresses another current LLM run during transfer. - Closure path: current LLM observes conversation → model selects transfertoagent → source worker drains outstanding output → runtime deactivates source and activates target without overlap → subsequent user-facing operation is produced by the selected target worker. - Boundary reachability: the behavior is implemented by first-party worker APIs and exercised by a runnable first-party multi-worker example whose documentation explicitly states that the LLM decides when to transfer. - Distinct S1 units: the active greeter/customer-service LLMWorker and the target support LLMWorker are separate model-driven operational workers sharing the conversation transport through the worker runtime. - Inter-S1 disturbance: without handoff drainage/exclusivity, the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, and both outputs can arrive interleaved. - Attenuating coordination relation: activateworker drains the source pipeline, deactivates it, then activates the selected target so the two workers do not simultaneously own user-facing output. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after the model-selected handoff, the target worker becomes active and receives/acts on subsequent conversation turns while the source is inactive. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism exists to attenuate a documented interference between two autonomous operational workers, and its result changes their subsequent operational participation; credit does not rely on WorkerBus messaging or delegation alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces safe switching, but the semantic choice of when/where to transfer is made by the active model from live conversational evidence. Removing that model decision leaves a callable handoff primitive, not the same coordination judgment. - Evidence: local-handoff-two-agents.py; worker.py; baseworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic WorkerBus messaging/job routing is not used as S2 evidence; credit depends on the concrete interleaving disturbance and the model-selected handoff closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current subordinate agent commitment while preserving a responsive voice/conversation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long-running delegated work can be idle, active, obsolete after a user correction, redirected, cancelled, failed or completed while the conversational front end continues receiving new input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from live subordinate-job state, choose whether to dispatch/update work, inspect current status or preemptively stop the active subordinate commitment. - Decision owner: the voice LLM in the first-party OpenClaw-agent composition; its context exposes sendtoagent, stopagent and agentstatus as model-callable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ActiveJob; bus job requests/updates/responses; job-group cancellation; WorkerRunner; OpenClaw worker; developer messages that return job outcomes to the voice model. - Closure path: voice LLM receives current user input plus returned job state/outcome → model chooses direct response or a current-control tool → Pipecat dispatches/updates, reports or cancels the subordinate job → bus update/response changes ActiveJob and is returned to the voice loop → later current-control decisions use the changed state. - Boundary reachability: the complete path is a runnable first-party multi-worker example built on public first-party worker/job APIs. - Whole-system current view: at the selected voice-plus-agent recursion, the voice regulator tracks the only subordinate operational commitment (jobid, request, start time and later response/update state) while continuing its own user-facing work. - Current-control decision scope: it can create/change the live delegated commitment and preempt it; this is stronger than read-only status or runtime lifecycle cleanup. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM itself selects the exposed dispatch/status/stop tools from current conversational evidence. Runtime code transports/enforces those choices; it does not make an equivalent semantic judgment when the model is removed. - Evidence: openclaw-agent/README.md; openclaw-agent.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the OpenClaw backend decides whether a forwarded update starts a new internal turn or steers an existing one. S3 credit is not assigned to that backend decision; it is assigned to Pipecat&#39;s model-facing authority to observe, issue/update and cancel the current subordinate commitment at this recursion. Generic WorkerRunner end/cancel behavior alone would be constructor lifecycle support, not agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently inspects current operational claims and returns findings into the live operation/current-control loop for corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pipecat can evaluate bot conversations and report assertion/judge failures, but those evaluation outcomes are separate test/eval results rather than a shipped production correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3 at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripted and simulated eval drivers; optional EvalJudge; expectation matching; pass/fail result objects; traces; ordinary worker job responses/errors. - Closure path: no S3-specific corrective closure established. Eval failures can stop or continue an evaluation scenario and are reported to the evaluator/caller, but the reviewed runtime does not feed a distinct auditor finding back into the audited bot&#39;s current work to require revision/retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate judge exists, but independence alone is insufficient; the missing production feedback closure prevents classification as A or C S3. - Evidence: scriptdriver.py; simulation.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can consume eval failures and implement corrective loops; caller composition is not credited as first-party Pipecat closure without shipped wiring.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established that converts environmental distinctions into persistent changes of Pipecat operating capability/policy used by later work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent conversation memory and dynamic current-session flow/context exist, but no reviewed mechanism closes prospective capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mem0 message storage/search; LLM conversation context; flow transitions and role messages; runtime LLM settings updates; session continuation. - Closure path: no S4-specific closure established. Mem0 stores user/assistant messages and retrieves relevant memories into later context; it does not itself decide or persist a changed skill/tool/policy/capability in response to external/future distinctions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory continuity and current-flow reconfiguration can affect future responses, but the reviewed first-party code does not contain the required sensing → prospective adaptation judgment → persistent capability change → reuse loop. - Evidence: memory.py; flows/manager.py; llmcontext.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream agent can use Pipecat tools to build its own learning/adaptation system; that is outside this standalone runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy loop with legitimate ultimate authority and returned closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bots can have durable system instructions, role messages and application-authored rules, but these are configured operating instructions rather than an S5 adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an S5 witness would require an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy issue to reach a legitimate parent/ultimate authority and the resulting policy decision to return into later operation; no such first-party path was established. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured systeminstruction; Flow rolemessage; tool schemas; application code; provider/session settings. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. Pipecat can apply or update instructions supplied by the application, but that is instruction transport/current configuration rather than ownership of identity or ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static developer-authored identity nor runtime system-instruction mutation by framework/application code establishes a legitimate ultimate-policy decision loop under Profile 0.2.4. - Evidence: flows/manager.py; llmservice.py; uiworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream product owners may govern bot identity out of band; this assessment does not convert arbitrary application configuration into Pipecat S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432/assessments/pipecat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432\">2f9af52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft AutoGen AgentChat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/tree/027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\">027ecf0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: stateful agents choose tools/actions and incorporate returned results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: team selection plus Magentic-One&#39;s explicit loop/stall detection and controlled transfer of the next action right suppress uncontrolled repetition/interference among multiple S1 participants. This is more than mere delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: MagenticOneOrchestrator owns a whole-team current facts/plan/progress ledger and has authority to assess progress, choose commitments, reset participants and regenerate the plan when the team stalls. Code-execution approval constrains an operational executor action rather than independently owning this whole-team current-control loop, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the progress ledger is part of the same operational S3 regulator that directs the team. It is not a distinct complementary audit channel with separate reviewing independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning after current-task stalls is inside-now adaptation. No distinct external/prospective intelligence function shaping future organizational posture is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: participant set, instructions, termination rules and ultimate authority remain application/parent-owned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/autogen-agentchat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data-to-paper</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper/tree/81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\">81df14c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform the environment-facing scientific work needed to transform project data and research state into executable analyses and research artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous datasets, generated-code errors, unexpected outputs/files, statistical-analysis requirements, intermediate product state and manuscript construction requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise analysis/code/content actions in response to current project products, execution results and validation/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party performer/code-writing agents in the hypothesis-testing workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage runner, CodeRunner, debugger conversers, code extract/check logic, output requirements, product store, data chaining and configured iteration bounds. - Closure path: current scientific products/data -&gt; agent proposes code/content action -&gt; first-party runner executes/materializes it -&gt; output/error/product state returns -&gt; agent revises or advances. - Boundary reachability: these paths are the shipped hypothesis-testing research workflow invoked by the installable application/CLI, not benchmark-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime and guardrails execute/check code, but the context-sensitive analysis/content choice and revision remain model-agent decisions. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py, requestcode.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the workflow contains multiple performer/reviewer roles and research stages, but no concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among distinct S1 units is identified and attenuated by a dedicated coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed stage transitions, product passing, paired conversations, deterministic sequencing and rewind/retry utilities. - Closure path: these mechanisms sequence work and transmit review feedback; they do not close a cross-S1 interference-regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interacting agents and state transfer are real, but the reviewed paths are production/review dependencies rather than S2-specific conflict attenuation. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system inside-and-now control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage completion, retries, rewinds, project parameters and review rounds constrain current work, but no first-party autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view plus broad authority over shared resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage flow and retry bounds are primarily encoded in deterministic runner logic and project parameters; local reviewers control product acceptance rather than organization-wide current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DataStepRunner/HypothesisTestingStepsRunner, stage enums, product readiness, retry/rewind helpers, project parameters and UI actions. - Closure path: configured stage/retry conditions alter control flow mechanically; no discretionary whole-system S3 owner is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents make local scientific/product decisions, while the broader workflow sequence is framework-defined. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide a separate challenge channel that reviews scientific/code/writing products and returns concrete corrective feedback before those products are accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible-but-wrong analysis outputs, code/result problems, weak hypotheses/plans, writing defects and other product claims that ordinary performer generation may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently approve a product or issue constructive concerns that force another performer revision cycle. - Decision owner: the separate reviewer LLM conversation in ReviewDialogDualConverserGPT and review-specific subclasses; in code paths, review agents inspect executed outputs/code products separately from the generating/debugging conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate ConversationManager, review termination phrase, max review rounds, product extraction/checks, code-review prompts and rewind utilities. - Closure path: performer produces valid candidate -&gt; reviewer receives the candidate/background -&gt; reviewer approves or supplies concerns -&gt; concerns are appended to the performer conversation with explicit correction request -&gt; performer rewrites/revises -&gt; review repeats. - Boundary reachability: the review dialog base class is reused by goal, planning and manuscript-writing review steps in the shipped hypothesis-testing workflow; code review is also embedded in the standard code-product path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is a separate agent conversation and owns the semantic approve/challenge judgment; deterministic loop code only routes/enforces that judgment. - Claim being audited: that a current scientific product/code result is adequate/correct enough to become accepted workflow state. - Ordinary reporting path: performer response plus normal generated/executed product state. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer conversation receives the product and relevant background/output context; code review can inspect created output contents. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own conversation manager/role and does not share the performer&#39;s assistant role; review feedback is generated independently and then returned. - Who acts on findings: the original performer/code-writing agent revises the product in the next cycle. - Evidence: dualconverser.py, requestcode.py, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: use external literature/novelty evidence to develop or revise future research direction and return the selected direction into the current experimental plan, with both autonomous and Copilot parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing literature may make a candidate goal non-novel, external findings may change relevant hypotheses, and a human scientist may judge the proposed future direction inadequate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in Autopilot, research/novelty agents judge literature-derived novelty and generate/refine the goal/hypothesis; in Copilot, human review can replace/supplement reviewer feedback and require revised research products. - Decision owner: autonomous research/reviewer agents in base mode; human scientist in the supported Copilot parent mode for the reviewed future-direction decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: literature-search agents, NoveltyAssessmentReview, ReGoalReviewGPT, stage return to GOAL, human-review application interactions, product storage and plan handoff. - Closure path: external literature -&gt; novelty/goal review -&gt; autonomous or parent judgment -&gt; if inadequate, workflow returns to goal generation/refinement -&gt; resulting goal/hypothesis/testing plan feeds data analysis and later research stages. - Boundary reachability: literature/novelty/goal refinement is part of the shipped hypothesis-testing runner, and README plus HumanReviewType/review interaction code expose Copilot human feedback through the same first-party workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode closes without human direction through model-agent novelty/refinement decisions; the separately supported Copilot mode intentionally transfers the relevant review/judgment right to the human and returns that decision into workflow state. - External distinction: retrieved scientific literature and most-similar-paper evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current research goal/hypothesis is novel/appropriate enough to warrant future experimentation. - Adaptation option generated: revised research goal/hypothesis and downstream hypothesis-testing plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: failed novelty sets regoal and returns to ScientificStage.GOAL; accepted/revised goal then feeds planning, code analysis and manuscript stages. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project parameters, research goals, human feedback and methodological guardrails constrain work, but no identity-level dispute is routed to an ultimate authority and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: startup/project parameters, user research goal, coding guardrails, reviewer prompts and human-review UI. - Closure path: these controls constrain local research behavior directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; returned policy loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous agents nor the Copilot human are shown resolving an identity-level policy issue at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/data-to-paper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:11:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CowAgent</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent/tree/7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\">7c55a61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled/delegated objectives into environment-facing tool/skill work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, model uncertainty, tool results/errors, files/shell/browser/service state, channel messages, and follow-up observations encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/skill action, incorporate returned observations, delegate bounded work when useful, or stop with a final response. - Decision owner: the running CowAgent Agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentStreamExecutor loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatService, AgentBridge, provider adapters, prompt/workspace construction, tool registry, permissions, session persistence, scheduler integration and context controls. - Closure path: each tool result, delegated result or new model observation returns into the first-party executor context and can change the next autonomous action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited executor/bridge/tool path is the normal shipped Web/channel runtime and is instantiated directly by standard CowAgent operation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code supplies transport, limits and execution, while the model actor owns the discretionary next-action choice on each loop iteration. - Evidence: pinned agent/chat/service.py and bridge/agentbridge.py; README.md corroborates the shipped planning/tool execution boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider/tool availability, permissions and iteration limits constrain the Agent&#39;s operational discretion but do not replace its next-action judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference between sibling operational Agents when concurrent delegations target the same persistent relay conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent handoffs to one source→target relay session would otherwise interleave turns in the target Agent&#39;s transcript and corrupt the shared conversational state used by later delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize admission to that target relay session so exactly one delegated turn occupies it at a time; timeout rather than permit an overlapping handoff when the lock cannot be acquired within policy. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the assessed boundary. The first-party S2-specific serialization primitive is complete and operational, but the conflict policy itself is deterministic/developer-selected rather than autonomously chosen by an Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-relay relaylock, stable relay-session derivation, delegation roster/ACL, depth/cycle checks, timeout and synchronous result return. - Closure path: a source Agent&#39;s delegation blocks on the relay lock; after the prior target turn finishes and releases the lock, the next delegated target run executes against a non-interleaved transcript and its result returns to the waiting source Agent, changing that source&#39;s subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: agentdelegate is a shipped team tool attached to normal team turns through AgentBridge; no downstream coordinator or test-only actor is required for the serialization path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents autonomously decide to delegate and select the teammate/task, but once two handoffs conflict the coordination choice is a fixed first-party lock policy. CowAgent therefore exposes a real S2-specific construction path without an autonomous owner of the conflict-regulation decision. - Evidence: pinned agent/tools/agentdelegate/agentdelegate.py and team-session support in bridge/agentbridge.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: other team features such as speaker selection, shared transcripts and ordinary handoff are not separately credited as S2 merely because they connect Agents. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured live CowAgent Agents with separate profiles/workspaces and first-party execution loops participating in one team conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping delegated turns to the same target relay session can interleave in the target transcript. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party per-relay lock queues competing handoffs and allows only one target turn at a time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordinated target result is returned synchronously to the delegating source Agent&#39;s tool loop; timeout/failure likewise returns a result that changes the source Agent&#39;s next decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock exists specifically because concurrent sibling handoffs would interfere by interleaving one operational transcript, and it changes admission/timing to suppress that identified disturbance before results feed back to the source Agent.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently configured CowAgent installation as a whole by changing which operational Agents are available, which models/capabilities they use, and which live channel instances/teams route work to them. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch between installed operational capacity and the operator&#39;s desired roster, agent availability, model/capability allocation, shared/own knowledge mode, default ownership and live channel/team bindings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update/archive/delete an Agent, make an Agent default, enable/disable it, alter its model/skills/knowledge allocation, or rebind a running channel/team to a different Agent roster. - Decision owner: the authenticated self-hosted operator is the legitimate parent actor in this supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentAdminService, roster revision checks, Web authentication, registry/router rebuilding, scheduler reconciliation, targeted live-runtime eviction and hot channel rebinding. - Closure path: the operator submits a first-party Web/API decision; CowAgent persists it, updates the live registry/router or channel binding, starts/stops the affected schedulers and evicts changed Agent instances so subsequent current work is executed under the returned control decision. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated Web console and its /api/agents administration paths are a standard shipped self-hosted operating surface documented as the unified configuration hub, not a development-only control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no standard first-party autonomous Agent is given installation-wide roster/model/capability/channel authority. The decisive current-control choice comes from the authenticated operator; runtime code only validates, persists and enforces it. - Evidence: pinned agent/admin.py, channel/web/api/agents.py, and bridge/agentbridge.py scheduler/runtime reconciliation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this claim is limited to installation-level current capability/resource control. Per-task steering/cancel and permission enforcement alone would not establish S3, and the assessment does not treat every settings edit as an S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: AgentAdminService.snapshot() exposes the installation&#39;s full current Agent roster, default Agent and channel instances; the same authenticated surface operates over that roster rather than one worker-local state. - Current-control decision scope: availability of S1 units, current model/capability allocation, default Agent, channel/team routing and scheduler participation for the running installation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established at the declared installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S3 witness; ordinary tool feedback, run/event recording and Self-Evolution review do not provide a materially independent route to operational reality for checking production claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution/tool events, conversation/run persistence, evolution records/backups, logs and operator inspection surfaces. - Closure path: no independent audit finding→corrective-current-control loop was established; recorded events and evolution changes serve their primary execution/adaptation purposes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no qualifying audit function whose decisive judgment could be assigned to an autonomous Agent. - Evidence: pinned chat/executor event path, conversation storage, Web/admin surfaces, and agent/evolution/ review path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could add an independent verifier or external monitor, but that composed system would be outside this frozen first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert externally observed conversation patterns and capability failures into prospective adaptations of reusable future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user preferences and recurring work patterns, skill defects discovered in real use, reusable workflows not yet encoded as capabilities, and unfinished work whose recurrence or omission would reduce future fit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after an idle conversation, decide autonomously whether there is a clear durable signal and whether to patch an existing skill, create a new reusable skill, consolidate knowledge/memory, or take no adaptation action. - Decision owner: the isolated first-party Self-Evolution review Agent/model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle trigger thresholds, restricted evolution toolset, guarded workspace paths, pre-change backup, snapshot diff/change detection, transactional rollback and evolution records. - Closure path: the review Agent senses conversation-derived external signals, selects and writes an adaptation, the executor commits only real guarded changes, and later normal Agent runs load/use the revised skill/knowledge/persona state; if no clear signal exists it explicitly leaves capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Self-Evolution is started by normal AgentBridge initialization, its configuration class defaults enabled, and the standard pinned config-template.json ships selfevolutionenabled: true; the decision actor is therefore reachable in the supported distribution without borrowing a dogfood or contributor agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger timing, backups and write guards are deterministic support, but the model review Agent owns the discretionary judgment of whether a past external interaction predicts future reusable need and which permitted adaptation to make. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/config.py, trigger.py, executor.py, prompts.py, bridge/agentbridge.py, config-template.json and docs/memory/self-evolution.mdx. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple memory consolidation alone is not the credited S4 witness. The positive mapping relies on the separately evidenced prospective skill-adaptation path: detecting a workflow likely to be wanted again or a skill defect exposed in use, autonomously generating an adaptation, and returning that change into later capability. - External distinction: the review consumes user conversation and actual skill/task experience from the environment, including repeated preferences, failures and emergent workflows. - Future / prospective distinction: the evolution prompt explicitly asks whether a workflow is reusable and likely to be wanted again, and documentation defines the goal as creating/fixing skills so they can be reused next time and future conversations improve. - Adaptation option generated: patch a defective skill, encode an emergent workflow as a new reusable skill, consolidate durable knowledge/memory, complete suitable unfinished work, or deliberately make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: committed workspace/skill/knowledge changes are the same first-party artifacts read by subsequent normal Agent operation; operator rollback remains a separate parent intervention path rather than the owner of the autonomous adaptation judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the Agent&#39;s durable identity/personality and highest-level workspace behavioral principles through first-party identity files that are loaded into subsequent system prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drift between the Agent&#39;s durable identity/behavioral principles and explicit repeated signals about what the assistant should be, plus explicit operator decisions to redefine those identity/policy artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the autonomous mode, decide whether an explicit repeated identity/personality/style signal is strong enough to justify a bounded AGENT.md identity revision; in the parent mode, authoritatively edit AGENT.md/RULE.md through the authenticated core-file administration surface. - Decision owner: base mode — the isolated Self-Evolution review Agent for the deliberately narrow autonomous identity-evolution decision; parent mode — the authenticated self-hosted operator as ultimate authority over the installation&#39;s core identity/policy files. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution signal criteria and guarded writes/backups; AgentAdminService core-file revision checks and atomic writes; authenticated Web endpoint; prompt/workspace loader; targeted runtime eviction after operator edits. - Closure path: autonomous mode: repeated identity-level external signal → review-Agent identity judgment → guarded AGENT.md edit → subsequent runtime loads the changed persona. Parent mode: authenticated operator reads/edits a core identity/policy file → atomic persistence and cached-Agent eviction → subsequent Agent instance reloads that file into its system prompt. - Boundary reachability: both modes are shipped supported runtime paths: default-enabled Self-Evolution may edit AGENT.md under its standard guard, while the authenticated Web Agents API exposes core-file read/write and immediately evicts the changed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base identity change is not a static prompt or automatic parser; the review Agent must judge whether a rare explicit repeated signal warrants changing its own identity artifact. The separate operator mode retains parent authority to set or override the same identity/policy surface, hence A(P) rather than plain A. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/prompts.py, executor.py, agent/prompt/workspace.py, agent/prompt/builder.py, agent/admin.py and channel/web/api/agents.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission settings, tool approvals, memory/preferences and per-task steering are not credited as S5. The autonomous claim is narrowly limited to the explicit first-party AGENT.md identity-evolution path; operator edits establish the separate parent mode. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: AGENT.md is explicitly defined by the shipped templates as the Agent&#39;s identity/personality and is loaded with RULE.md into the system-prompt context; this is materially different from ordinary task or tool policy. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — Self-Evolution Agent decides whether the standard rare-signal criterion warrants a durable identity edit; parent (P) — authenticated operator directly owns the authoritative core-file edit and may override/undo Agent evolution. - Return-to-operation path: both modes persist the core file used by prompt construction; operator writes additionally evict the cached runtime, and later Agent initialization reloads the changed identity/policy context.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4/assessments/cowagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4\">a00011a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agno</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/tree/44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\">44219f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: member agents autonomously execute bounded model/tool work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: TeamMode.tasks provides an out-of-box agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop over a shared task board. The leader assigns ownership, prevents duplicate tasks, encodes dependencies, observes member outcomes and can retry/reassign/change the plan; dependency state prevents conflicting/premature execution. This goes beyond one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the same leader has a current whole-team view of tasks, assignees, dependency/blocked state, results and failures, plus authority to create/reprioritize through dependencies, assign/reassign members, execute work, update status and decide overall completion. That is a concrete agent-owned current-control loop over shared operational commitments. Human-in-the-loop paths pause member/tool execution and return the result to the team; they do not independently take ownership of the team-level assignment/plan/completion right, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Agno lets applications compose reviewer/critic members, but the standard team runtime does not supply a distinct sufficiently independent audit channel with alternative operational access and required corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: memory, knowledge, reasoning and learning capabilities can improve team operation but do not by themselves instantiate a distinct external-and-prospective intelligence function coupled against S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: team role, instructions, permissions, approvals and identity are application/parent configured. The tasks leader regulates execution but is not given ultimate agent-owned policy/identity authority or an S3–S4 closure role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/agno.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:41:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kuLm1Fs/lumak-ts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LumaK</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kuLm1Fs/lumak-ts/tree/ef1d2ddb16630e537c205fadb11031ba7bae6131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ef1d2ddb16630e537c205fadb11031ba7bae6131\">ef1d2dd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Use workspace tools to answer a coding task and, when authorized, change code. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous user goals, workspace state, tool errors, context pressure and permission decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select navigation, file/tool actions, delegation and revisions in response to observed results. - Decision owner: The active model-backed Agent owns the semantic tool and revision choices. Operator permission constrains certain actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkspaceGuard, provider adapters, tool registry, dedup cache, role modes, session store and traces. - Closure path: Task → model request → selected workspace tool → result or denial enters messages → subsequent action or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: CLI, embedded TUI and gateway instantiate Agent. Supported auto mode permits the loop to choose and apply local edits within the workspace. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing the next investigation or edit is model-owned. The runtime constrains and executes those choices. - Evidence: apps/cli/src/commands/chat.ts, packages/core/src/agent.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/loop.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/tool-call.ts, packages/core/src/providers/factory.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct response to lateral interference between independent coding units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent children can share a workspace and issue overlapping operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Observe an actual cross-child conflict and coordinate a subsequent change in both affected operations. - Decision owner: Main model delegates tasks; the runtime executes child calls concurrently and joins results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Promise.all, parent permission ceiling, read-only roles and per-call tool execution. - Closure path: Children return outcomes to the parent. The shipped scheduling relation does not bind sibling conflict detection and adaptive redistribution. - Boundary reachability: Agent registry supplies the delegate tool. Child loops have no recursive delegate path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegation, capability limits and a final join support the parent task without defining a lateral conflict-resolution loop. - Evidence: packages/core/src/runtime/loop.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/delegate.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/permissions.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current-control mode is established across coding sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Active session errors, tool requests, workspace selection and availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Intervene in whole-system resource commitments using a current operating view and returned decision. - Decision owner: Operator governs a local session and tools. The primary agent delegates its own work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gateway conversation and trace views, approval prompts, loop limits and cancellation. - Closure path: Approvals or changed modes affect specific calls or sessions. - Boundary reachability: Gateway supports session and project state operations, while Agent handles each turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current workspace and per-call permissions do not form a portfolio-control decision or feedback relation. - Evidence: packages/gateway/src/server.ts, packages/gateway/src/state.ts, packages/gateway/src/chat.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/permissions.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement the producing agent&#39;s own work report with a separate read-only inspection of code changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Latent implementation defects or gaps that remain after the producing agent&#39;s own tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect the target files and judge whether the implementation contains issues worth correction. - Decision owner: The separately instantiated reviewer model makes the review judgment. The parent model decides whether and how to revise. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in delegate tool, reviewer-specific prompt, role-bound read-only tools, shared workspace and returned DelegateReport. - Closure path: Primary model delegates review → fresh reviewer reads source files and reports → answer is appended as a tool result → parent model may edit or report in its next step. - Boundary reachability: Default Agent registry mounts delegate; auto permits invocation and approve can permit it. The reviewer prompt is shipped by the core prepack configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The child chooses its own read actions and findings in a fresh review context. The parent cannot substitute its own work report for that separate tool-backed review within this path. - Claim being audited: Whether selected existing code or recent edits fulfill the implementation intent without defects relevant to the delegated review. - Ordinary reporting path: The primary session observes its edit results, tool outcomes and its own response. - Complementary access path: The role-specific child can read and search the shared workspace directly, with a fresh prompt and without edit permissions. - Independence boundary: Separate child loop and prompt with read-only access. It uses the same configured provider and filesystem, and the parent chooses scope and contextual framing. - Who acts on findings: The primary model reads the returned reviewer answer and can adjust implementation or report findings. The harness does not mandate review or enforce a verdict. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/delegate.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/prompts.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/permissions.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/loop.ts, packages/core/package.json, prompts/subagent-reviewer.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for reachability and separate artifact access; medium for practical independence under shared provider and parent-selected scope. - Caveats: Conditional on a reviewer call with suitable tools and a code artifact to inspect. Static source demonstrates the corrective return path, not measured effectiveness or mandatory remediation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective capability adaptation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace changes, context overflow and availability of existing skills or provider tools. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Observe future environmental change and select a durable adaptation returned to current capability. - Decision owner: The operator supplies skill files and provider configuration; the runtime selects available inputs for the current task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skills selector, context truncation, optional MCP and persisted session memory. - Closure path: A matching skill or retrieved context alters the current prompt. - Boundary reachability: Built-in skill matching, session resume and provider selection are reachable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting stored guidance and reacting to current input are local operational behaviors. - Evidence: packages/core/src/runtime/loop.ts, packages/core/src/skills/selector.ts, packages/core/src/skills/store.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/session.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity or policy arbitration is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current tool permissions and configured role boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Arbitrate system mandate or ultimate policy and return a binding decision. - Decision owner: The operator sets the tool mode and workspace; developer-defined role rules enforce limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI modes, approval gates and child permission ceiling. - Closure path: Per-action approval and policy selection change what may run in the current session. - Boundary reachability: Explicit CLI/Gateway approval and plan modes are shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: User consent to an action and a constructor policy do not establish a first-party ultimate identity decision process. - Evidence: apps/cli/src/commands/chat.ts, packages/gateway/src/chat.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/permissions.ts, packages/core/src/runtime/delegate.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b29fcd0709bdf141f7b15f579d4f05a57ad585ff/assessments/lu/lumak.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b29fcd0709bdf141f7b15f579d4f05a57ad585ff\">b29fcd0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chenofchina/onion-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Onion Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chenofchina/onion-harness/tree/dc2be116eddad9c45018d0d8590fa6cc96387bfe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dc2be116eddad9c45018d0d8590fa6cc96387bfe\">dc2be11 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: first-party hosted model agents interpret user tasks and perform tool or deliberative work in local environments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user goals, tool results, file/task conditions and differing model-seat arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed agent chooses next task-level action/tool call or substantive seat reply/rebuttal from prompt and feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed agent actor in normal mode; individual seat agents in Roundtable. The deterministic Onion application and external engine transport the choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EngineLoop, provider adapters, executor registry, sandbox, Plan/Standard/Strict mode, approvals, memory, task events and configured ceilings. - Closure path: agent-selected call reaches an Onion executor, tool result returns to engine context for further agent choice; Roundtable seat replies enter the shared transcript and later rounds/final consensus. - Boundary reachability: OnionRuntime.runtask wires live tools/options into EngineLoop.run; the API constructs and starts Roundtable in a supported application path, rather than only in tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-dependent action and substantive reasoning disappear with the agent actor, while retaining Onion&#39;s deterministic mechanics; the latter do not independently choose the goal&#39;s actions. - Evidence: runtime loop, tool creation and feedback, Roundtable turn/feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped model-backed action/seat loops; no claim of successful paid-provider E2E execution in this review. - Caveats: the internal loongagent dependency is not separately assessed; local app uses its installed engine and a configured model rather than a first-party foundation model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Roundtable reduces blocking disagreements among independent model-seat contributions to produce a coherent joint deliberative outcome or a bounded explicit divergence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incompatible seat stances and directed rebuttals can prevent the meeting from converging. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-backed host chooses the first seat per round after reading the evolving transcript and outstanding disagreement; each seat can revise/rebut from shared feedback. - Decision owner: host model agent for the discretionary next-speaker selection; other seat agents own their subsequent replies. Deterministic consensus/budget computation does not inherit that discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared transcript, validated seat IDs, fixed tail speaking order, agreement/rejection parsing, round/call/token ceilings and human breaker. - Closure path: conflict appears as rejected stances/rebuttals; the host reads the transcript and selects the next first speaker, seats read preceding positions on later turns; consensus or the recorded divergences determines whether to stop, continue or involve the human. - Boundary reachability: the user-facing Roundtable API creates an actual RoundtableOrchestrator, while speakingorder makes a live host model call on its first-party normal execution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removal of host deliberation leaves only deterministic rotation, not the dispute-aware speaker choice; mechanical ordering and consensus calculation remain supporting machinery. The host&#39;s returned focus field is not presently forwarded as a direct seat instruction, so the positive witness is bounded to first-speaker selection plus visible transcript/replies, not a stronger agenda-control claim. - Distinct S1 units: 2–6 model-backed seats with separate replies and optional rebuttal targets. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concrete disagreeing stances/rebuttals about the shared topic, which block consensus. - Attenuating coordination relation: host selection uses the transcript and prompts priority to whoever can address the open disagreement; subsequent seats see the accumulated positions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: next round&#39;s host prompt and seat prompts include preceding contributions, and the consensus result stops/continues the meeting. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a real conflict representation (rejections/targeted rebuttals) is tied to disagreement-aware host choice and a next-round response path; generic message passing alone is not the credited function. - Evidence: host and seat prompts, host choice, rebuttal and consensus path, verdict implementation, rebuttal/breaker tests. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; source proves a dispute-specific feedback design, not observed convergence improvement under live provider conditions. - Caveats: this is S2 in supported Roundtable mode, not in an ordinary single-agent session. The host model&#39;s optional close and focus JSON fields are not the decisive mechanical closure of a round.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent/operator supervision of the current Roundtable&#39;s resource composition and commitments across its model-seat cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: debate stalls, insufficient specialist coverage, divergent statements or unsatisfactory current answer distribution across seats. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the human operator chooses the meeting roster/model constraints at creation and can add a new seat, address one or all seats, and return a new directive while the meeting is underway or after the breaker. - Decision owner: the local human/operator at the application/meeting parent boundary; model host selection alone does not establish agent-owned whole-system S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: meeting status/roster/minutes, API mediation, capped seat count, inbox, event persistence, model-cost and breaker display. - Closure path: a user-added seat joins the next meeting round; a targeted message enters that seat&#39;s inbox and is delivered at its next speaking turn; post-meeting human input reopens a bounded follow-up without silently clearing prior consensus. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Roundtable UI/API connects operator messages and seat addition to the same live orchestrator and its subsequent rounds. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model host cannot decide to create extra provider seats or authoritatively change the operator&#39;s live constraints; these are returned human current-control decisions. Generic cancel and destructive-command approval are not separately counted as whole-system S3. - Whole-system current view: Roundtable&#39;s member roster, statements, per-seat usage, divergences and minutes are available to the user across its current operational seats. - Current-control decision scope: parent choices over team composition, targeted priorities and intervention within the bounded meeting; not an inferred global allocator across independent Onion tasks. - Evidence: Roundtable UI contract, human messages/follow-up, live seat addition, inbox return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; the positive parent claim is bounded to the current Roundtable, not whole installation-wide budgeting. - Caveats: the operator can be human and does not need to be modeled as an AI agent; no autonomous S3 resource/commitment discretion is established merely from timeouts/host ordering.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: a separate audit would have to compare ordinary operational success/claims against independent underlying reality and return findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false reported completion, unsound execution, unobserved side effects or unverifiable model claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a distinct first-party complementary operational audit right in the reviewed modes. - Decision owner: none evidenced for such a distinct audit; the optional safety-review model judges proposed code before execution, and the user can approve a flagged request, but neither audits a claimed completed operational outcome through another access path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: code-only safety filter, Plan mode refusal, user confirmation, diff view, tests and logs. - Closure path: no first-party complementary direct-reality audit finding is wired to corrective control as a separate function. Safety-review verdicts affect the ordinary executor&#39;s pre-execution gate only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an isolated reviewer model is not an S3 owner without a complementary audit of operating reality; authors explicitly call it a filter, not final authority. - Evidence: review-gate invocation, code-only review, README warning. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the shipped runtime/reviewer/GUI paths inspected. - Caveats: no universal claim about unexamined third-party plugins or a user&#39;s own independent verification procedure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: parent-governed prospective adaptation of the agent&#39;s future available model/tool/skill capabilities in response to demands from the external user/work environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new tool/provider/skill needs beyond the currently enabled local repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the working agent can formulate a reasoned future-capability change proposal, but only the legitimate local human/operator decides whether it changes the installed repertoire. - Decision owner: human parent for adoption of the future capability; no automatic agent-owned S4 closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MetaTools proposal API, dry-run ledger diff, capability validation and versioning, MCP tool materialization and hot reload. - Closure path: user-supplied/newly encountered capability need reaches the agent prompt; agent may propose a new executor/provider/skill/MCP tool; parent applies the proposal; the next runtime constructs its tools/prompt from the revised state or hot-loaded MCP registry. - Boundary reachability: meta tools are added to the normal agent&#39;s live tool registry and afterledgerchange updates the shipped runtime, including persisted tool availability; this is not merely a developer roadmap or test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: only the parent approval changes capability. Counterfactually removing the parent leaves proposals un-applied and does not close adaptation; this is not S4=A. - External distinction: changing user tasks and available model/tool services create a capability gap that can be expressed through the tool&#39;s mandatory reason and proposal content; no autonomous external scanning is claimed. - Future / prospective distinction: requested changes alter tools/providers/skills for subsequent actions or sessions, not merely the immediate task answer. - Adaptation option generated: agent-proposed registration of an MCP tool, model-provider preset, executor or skill. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved ledger state is re-read by OnionRuntime / refreshed MCP tools and becomes an available action for subsequent S1 execution. - Evidence: default operating prompt, meta tool proposals, human apply/reload. - Basis: structural for shipped proposal/return; inferred for the external-demand-to-adaptation interpretation. - Confidence: medium-low. This is a supported parent-closed capability adaptation mode, not evidence of autonomous environmental forecasting or systematic S4 strategy. - Caveats: a generic user-chosen plugin is not S4 by itself; this positive claim is limited to the model-originated, reasoned future capability proposal and actual parent return path. No source trace establishes recurring environmental surveillance or measured adaptation effectiveness.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: parent-governed ultimate change to the agent&#39;s core identity and operating rules, rather than an ordinary task/tool approval. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension between the current core behavior/policy and a desired enduring operating identity for future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator authoritatively accepts/rejects a model- or user-originated update to the persistent core system-prompt segment. - Decision owner: legitimate local human operator of the self-hosted agent, not the proposing agent or deterministic ledger. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: onionupdatesystemprompt, ChangeLedger validation/diff/version history, API confirmation and ordered system-channel prompt assembly. - Closure path: core operating-rule proposal reaches confirmation UI; operator calls apply/reject; accepted segment is persisted; composesystemprompt and OnionRuntime.composesystemprompt inject it into future model calls. - Boundary reachability: normal agent tool registry contains the prompt-update meta tool, and user-facing API applies proposals to live state read on every later task. No adjacent maintainer CI or unshipped governance actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model can suggest but cannot activate its own enduring identity/policy change. No separate autonomous S5 ultimate-authority mode is established. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the core segment begins with You are Onion and durable operating rules; changing that segment changes the identity-level instructions inherited by future tasks, distinguishable from approval of a particular shell command. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: only human parent mode positively established; autonomous policy proposal is not decisive authority. - Return-to-operation path: ledger apply persists the approved segment; the runtime composes the system channel from that state on the next run. - Evidence: core identity and policy, prompt-change proposal, parent application, runtime system-channel return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped parent closure; the grade does not imply the default agent authors its own constitutional rules. - Caveats: a generic per-tool confirmation is not counted as S5. This applies only when the human-authorized change affects the durable identity/core policy segment.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d4f179b0c40a972a750d2e91fbef975709c36d2/assessments/on/onion-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d4f179b0c40a972a750d2e91fbef975709c36d2\">2d4f179 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:52:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sakomws/slack-agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Slack Agent Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sakomws/slack-agent-runtime/tree/16bb262715b7bacb64c7466cf106063c2fdf6c93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 16bb262715b7bacb64c7466cf106063c2fdf6c93\">16bb262 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out bounded conversation/MCP actions and coding tasks based on model-selected next actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unstructured requests, tool replies, changing code/tests and model errors within a bounded assigned job. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose subsequent MCP tool or coding workspace operation after observing results, and choose when to finish. - Decision owner: the first-party agent/tool and coding-worker loops supplied with an external inference model; the user selects the objective and the service bounds permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: step budget, tool schema, WorkspaceTools, approval broker, allowlists, isolated checkout and separated publication credentials. - Closure path: Slack request or dispatched coding task → inference decision → tool execution → result injected into model conversation → next action or final result/PR artifact. - Boundary reachability: default Slack MCP path and the shipped allowlisted coding-job entrypoint invoke real first-party loops; no custom developer agent implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool choice and iterative repair are model-discretionary, while approval and final publish are enforced by non-model layers. - Evidence: MCP agent loop, coding worker, tools. - Basis: implemented structural flow; no live inference run. - Confidence: high for agent-loop ownership, not for actual task success. - Caveats: the GitHub publication step uses separate credentials, and a human is required for merge; neither is credited as agent decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party regulation of interference among distinct autonomous S1 coding units at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential concurrent jobs writing to overlapping branches or projects, but no evidenced S2-specific coordination loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent for inter-S1 collision resolution; admins choose work requests, while quotas/locks execute fixed limits. - Decision owner: no autonomous S2-specific coordinator identified in shipped runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread state, active task tracking, policy quotas, repository allowlisting, fixed GitHub Actions dispatch. - Closure path: no first-party conflict signal → discretionary coordination → altered subsequent S1 behavior chain evidenced; isolated task guards are not this chain. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduling/rate limiting does not demonstrate mediation of an inter-agent conflict. - Evidence: policy, coding dispatch, spec limitations. - Basis: bounded no-path after checking shipped dispatch, policy and coordination surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high within deployed runtime boundary. - Caveats: upstream GitHub may serialize or reject changes, but that is not first-party discretionary inter-S1 coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-installation discretionary present-operational supervision demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current job failures, quota saturation and review/dispatch state are exposed but not used in a first-party whole-system control loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped actor chooses portfolio priorities, reallocates work or changes current operational commitments from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: human administrators issue independent commands; a programmatic S3 owner is not established by their local job actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable Actions job-status queries, per-thread state and quotas, policy and stream cancellation. - Closure path: individual code status/review status queries return observations to requesters; no demonstrated whole-system current-view → supervisory choice → altered work portfolio path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current status reporting and deterministic quota enforcement are not discretionary whole-system control. - Evidence: technical specification, thread coordinator, coding plugin. - Basis: bounded source-static absence. - Confidence: medium-high for ordinary shipped modes. - Caveats: external human operations could build supervision on top; not counted without a first-party return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: review the correctness, reliability and security of a proposed code change through a model judgment separate from the coding worker. - Disturbance / variety regulated: builder omissions and errors that pass self-report or initial local tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: report structured findings and select a validated severity-derived REQUESTCHANGES or APPROVE recommendation. - Decision owner: separately dispatched PullRequestReviewer model workflow; publication safety checks can downgrade its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source diff extraction, exact SHA, JSON validation, check status, GitHub review publication and human merge authority. - Claim being audited: the submitted PR satisfies correctness, security and reliability requirements; reviewer does not merely summarize coding worker&#39;s completion. - Ordinary reporting path: worker summary, test report and PR diff/CI status. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives actual PR diff plus title/body with separate inference instructions, producing findings by file/line independently of worker self-report. - Independence boundary: reviewer is a distinct invocation with its own role and bounded input; same underlying provider/model or stronger institutional independence is not established. - Who acts on findings: review workflow publishes a permitted GitHub review or comment; maintainers and authors correct the change, with required checks/merge authority retained externally. - Closure path: PR head/diff → independently invoked model reviewer → validated findings/severity → GitHub review/comment → author/maintainer action before merge. - Boundary reachability: the shipped review &lt;repo&gt;#&lt;PR&gt; Slack command dispatches the review workflow; it is an opt-in built-in mode, not automatically run for every coding job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer generates an evaluative model judgment; deterministic review-event selection and publishing restrictions only constrain it. - Evidence: reviewer code, review plugin, review workflow architecture. - Basis: implemented separate review mode, source-static. - Confidence: medium-high in structural complementarity; review efficacy and provider independence untested. - Caveats: no automatic closed builder-fix/re-review loop proven; closure relies on normal GitHub author/maintainer feedback, so the mode&#39;s end-to-end effectiveness remains unverified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party longer-horizon scanning/adaptation decision path for the runtime&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prospective changes in user needs, provider/service ecosystem and platform behavior are mentioned in documents but not regulated by an operating adaptation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no installed agent chooses future runtime strategy/options against external signals and returns them into current capability. - Decision owner: maintainers can revise roadmap outside the assessed runtime; no shipped S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: inference web search for user tasks, roadmap, issue-oriented coding worker and spec review. - Closure path: normal user-requested coding can change external repositories; it is S1 task delivery, not foresight about this runtime&#39;s identity/capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: web search and document review serve immediate requests, not a prospective environment-to-adaptation loop. - Evidence: roadmap, README, technical spec. - Basis: bounded no-path for shipped first-party modes. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: operator-maintained roadmap is not attributed to a deployed agent or parent-governed runtime S4 without return-to-operation evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party ultimate mission/identity decision and return loop at the runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: role/credential access boundaries are protected, but the system&#39;s purpose/ultimate policy is not decided by the installed runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent or explicit parent policy path authoritatively revises the runtime&#39;s ultimate mission and binds later operation. - Decision owner: humans manage configuration, whitelists, deployment and merge permissions as local operational governance, not a proved S5 identity-mode closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: admin IDs, repository allowlists, MCP approval, credentials, model choice and separated privileged publication stages. - Closure path: local policy configuration gates requests; it does not demonstrate an ultimate-policy decision circuit, nor make approval requests S5 by default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission checks and human approvals enforce task scope without selecting a durable system identity. - Evidence: policy module, technical spec, approval broker. - Basis: bounded absence, not a judgment on the human maintainer organization&#39;s actual policy. - Confidence: medium; no runtime identity decision path identified. - Caveats: configuration policy can reflect human values; this alone does not prove the required ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2c490e2b9da35239ab17f1fd10a4f48c686b3367/assessments/sl/slack-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2c490e2b9da35239ab17f1fd10a4f48c686b3367\">2c490e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:55:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bondie/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness (ticket team)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bondie/agent-harness/tree/6c011629ecf3f0cb2cfd72084502e58a31ad3206\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6c011629ecf3f0cb2cfd72084502e58a31ad3206\">6c01162 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed analysis, effort estimation and approved ticket-specific code changes in a target project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable tracker requirements, codebase structure, acceptance criteria, failing tests, reference-ticket evidence and QA findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The analyst/estimator interpret ticket content and choose analysis/forecast; the implementer chooses substantive code edits and next local repair from test or review feedback within the approved task. - Decision owner: Instantiated Claude Code subagent models as operational actors, explicitly invoked by the installed ticket skill; human owns the scope gate, not code-level reasoning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task-specific agent instructions, project-test post-edit hook, configurable precedent MCP, approval gate and tracker-write guard. - Closure path: Ticket → analyst/estimator choices → human-approved implementation → model-selected edits → repository/test observations → corrections → QA result and handover. - Boundary reachability: /agent-harness:ticket is the advertised installed entry point and explicitly names the subagents; the implementer definition grants Read/Write/Edit/Bash and receives the project test hook when configured. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the task-operating models and the prompt/hook package cannot interpret tickets or produce discretionary code changes; the host executes the tool choices but does not replace the task-local reasoning right. - Evidence: ticket skill, implementer, test feedback. - Basis: structural first-party installed workflow. - Confidence: High for defined agent decision roles; medium for live host behavior not independently exercised. - Caveats: Requires a configured external Claude Code runtime, tracker and target project; offline estimator experiments are not equivalent to live plugin decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced inter-operational-unit interference attenuation loop, distinct from sequential work handoff. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A ticket can produce analyst/estimator/implementer disagreement, but no peer S1 oscillation or simultaneous incompatible commitments are detected and damped by a separate coordinator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The lead follows a prescribed sequence and human gate; it has no evidenced S2-specific adjustment right over independent peer operations. - Decision owner: Lead role for local routing and human for plan changes; no separately demonstrated S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fixed role sequence, approval before implementation, bounded QA cycles and role write restrictions. - Closure path: Task outputs pass to the next role; no qualifying peer-conflict signal → coordinating choice → adjusted peer behavior loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegating roles and handing a result forward is not by itself regulation of interaction-generated operational variety. - Evidence: installed skill steps. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Other multi-team integrations are outside this ticket-team boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated discretionary whole-system present-control allocation or operational-portfolio commitment loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong estimates, implementation failures, failed tests and review findings are local task matters, not a cross-unit resource/commitment picture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human approves scope and external writes; lead gates a prescribed ticket sequence. No agent legitimately revises whole-system operational allocations. - Decision owner: Human for plan and write approval, implementer for task-local edits, prescribed skill for sequencing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human AskUserQuestion before implementation, two QA correction rounds, configurable test command, tracker MCP write guard. - Closure path: Gate → approved individual ticket → implementation/review/handover; no cross-unit performance consolidation and current-control reallocation return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead&#39;s management label and task handoffs do not establish S3 portfolio authority at this recursion. - Evidence: ticket skill, tracker guard. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human manager of multiple unrelated tickets could implement an external S3 that this package does not define.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Distinct independent read-only QA inspection of implementation assertions before human handover. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer claims of task completion despite erroneous code, missed acceptance criteria, missing tests or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The reviewer chooses approve or changes requested and reports verified path:line defects plus checks and criterion status independently of the implementer&#39;s report. - Decision owner: Separate reviewer-qa Claude Code subagent; the lead receives its verdict and routes requested corrections to the implementer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit review step, reviewer disallowedTools: Write, Edit, NotebookEdit, direct git diff/git status and code-context reading, independent test execution and up to two corrective rounds. - Closure path: Implementer reports completion → distinct reviewer checks working-tree artifacts and runs validation → issue-specific verdict → lead sends defects back to implementer → new implementation and re-review → human sees evidence/verdict. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer&#39;s substantive assessment/verdict is agent judgment on direct source/test evidence; the skill-owned orchestration returns it to the lead and influences completion. This is separate from ordinary implementer self-tests or a deterministic linter. - Claim being audited: Completed changes meet the ticket analysis and every acceptance criterion without production defects. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer summary of files, tests and criteria returned to the lead after edits. - Complementary access path: Reviewer independently reads the actual uncommitted diff, status and surrounding files and executes read-only tests/linters/static checks. - Independence boundary: Separate named model subagent without edit tools; cannot edit audited code or merely reuse implementer self-report. It remains subject to the same provider/host and its invocation depends on lead compliance with installed skill instructions. - Who acts on findings: Lead routes verified QA findings into an implementer rework round and withholds final handover until the bounded review process completes or escalates to the human. - Boundary reachability: The installed primary /ticket skill explicitly calls reviewer-qa after implementer and reruns it on change requests; the reviewer definition prescribes primary-artifact inspection, a verdict and line-level findings. - Evidence: independent reviewer, review and correction steps, implementer report path. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party audit path. - Confidence: High for wired separation and corrective instructions, medium for compliance in unobserved live sessions. - Caveats: Prompt-level reviewer separation, not a cryptographically isolated verifier or automatic no-bypass acceptance gate. Claims of tests passing do not replace actual project test command provisioning.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous prospective adaptation of the ticket-team&#39;s organizational capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Historical ticket variability and changing complexity are used for current-task forecasting, not future operating-model change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Estimator forecasts current ticket hours from precedents; no owned decision to revise future team tools, policy, capacity or capability. - Decision owner: Estimator for this task&#39;s prediction, user/developer for future plugin design and installed configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Historical precedent MCP and estimate P25–P75, offline model-evaluation scripts, explicit approved approach. - Closure path: Past tickets → estimate for the current task → human plan decision; no environment futures → strategic adaptation decision → operating-organization feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reference-class forecasting is anticipatory about this ticket&#39;s effort, but does not itself establish S4 future-environment/capability adaptation. - Evidence: estimation step, estimator role. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External team planning informed by ticket forecasts could be a separate S4 outside this plugin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced first-party ultimate-purpose, organizational identity or constitutional arbitration loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky code changes, tracker writes, plan deviations and unapproved commits are governed at an operational authorization level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may approve implementation and individual write actions; no actor resolves a dispute about the team&#39;s enduring ultimate policy or identity. - Decision owner: Human requester for plan and action consent, installed rules/hooks for routine constraints; no packaged S5 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit AskUserQuestion plan gate, no commit/push without human consent, subagent tracker-write denial hook. - Closure path: Proposed action → approval or denial → execute/stop; no identity/purpose dispute → legitimate policy-authority judgment → enduring organizational policy revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only write restrictions and per-action consent protect execution boundaries, not an S5 policy identity feedback loop. - Evidence: human gates, tracker guard. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Host global instructions and organizational identity policies reside outside this plugin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1ec53be3a1853bb4d51e279e16f4b959fe32b354/assessments/ag/agent-harness-ticket-team.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1ec53be3a1853bb4d51e279e16f4b959fe32b354\">1ec53be &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:18:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/berkesecicii/free-multi-agent-ai-council\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">JARVIS AI Council</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/berkesecicii/free-multi-agent-ai-council/tree/20a93de357da82c39255932da71dfb12aeea2f49\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 20a93de357da82c39255932da71dfb12aeea2f49\">20a93de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: attached specialist agents independently execute coding, web-research, reading or review tasks and return task results and observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: codebase state, provider tool results, user questions, web evidence and unexpected errors within each assigned specialist responsibility. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role model decides its research or code steps, tool requests and content after observing context/tool outputs; host controls access approval and routing. - Decision owner: selected model-backed specialist (VISION/Claude CLI, FRIDAY/Codex, EDITH/Gemini or other installed supported agent), not the deterministic Python routing rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLIs/adapters, project-command/patch/search, browser/desktop bridge, role-scoped chat context, saved task/output state and human permissions. - Closure path: user/JARVIS assigns task → first-party launcher reaches signed-in provider agent and approved tools → model consumes tool observations and delivers artifact/findings → host records output → later team stage or user consumes it. - Boundary reachability: providers are explicitly wired by first-party adapters and runtime launch; team runner selects their actual connector and creates a bounded child chat/task rather than only naming hypothetical specialists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider-backed agents choose task-specific actions after observations, while local approval prompts and quotas do not themselves create agent discretion. - Evidence: shipped connectors and task semantics; task launch; live provider launching. - Basis: source-reachable supported model execution modes. - Confidence: medium-high for structure; external paid/subscription models not exercised. - Caveats: the first-party host cannot prove the quality of any third-party proprietary model&#39;s internal reasoning.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: first-party grouping and connector-busy guards attenuate incompatible overlapping work across specialist runs while returning completed producer context to a later specialist. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous role workers attempting the same constrained provider connector, or a dependent role acting before a producer output exists. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human/model step plans indicate dependencies or independent stages; host accepts at most two same-group jobs and refuses two concurrent uses of a connector; it defers dependent stages until the current group&#39;s work is collected. - Decision owner: static coordinator constructor and plan authors, not an agent capable of negotiating runtime contention or autonomously repairing a conflicted schedule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: modeljobs provider-busy check, threading.Lock, same-connector rejection, parallel grouping, grouping barriers, bounded prior-stage packet, and failure/cancellation statuses. - Closure path: candidate parallel group is tested for connector conflict → only nonconflicting roles launch; the next group waits for current completion → collected result is returned as bounded context to subsequent role; if provider already busy, the team exits with explicit error instead of colliding. - Boundary reachability: teamrunner executes these checks directly before invoking first-party configured model adapters, and persists the workflow state visible to the user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interference attenuation is specific to distinct S1 agents sharing provider capacity and result dependencies; its selection policy is configured/deterministic, requiring further autonomous composition for S2=A. - Distinct S1 units: enabled EDITH research, VISION coding and FRIDAY review/development workers operating in the same team request. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping role calls compete for shared provider capacity; a consumer role can act on an incomplete producer result. - Attenuating coordination relation: connector-distinct parallel group admission and completed-group barrier with packet return. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict prevents launch and surfaces team error; completion unlocks subsequent stage and provides its result, so downstream work waits for producer evidence. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is runtime prevention of a specific conflicting simultaneous provider use plus an explicit inter-role completion barrier, not the fact that prompts mention other agents. - Evidence: group building, connector checks and barrier; shared runtime job and quota guards. - Basis: first-party structural constructor path. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no agent-level merge conflict resolution or general distributed work scheduling is claimed; configured write permissions/isolated chats prevent some interactions without solving arbitrary semantic conflicts.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: JARVIS assigns present team work to available specialists and constrains who executes next; in a separate supervised mode the human approves and starts a proposed multi-role plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatch between request and expertise, specialist availability/quota, excessive handoff depth and pending current workload. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in automatic chat mode the JARVIS model chooses to hand an actionable brief to another specialist, and host executes it within a two-handoff current chain; in explicit plan mode the operator decides whether the proposed role/effort/step commitments begin. - Decision owner: autonomous JARVIS coordinator model in the active auto-handoff configuration; legitimate Mac user/operator in the first-party reviewed-plan and direct-call configurations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role/connector availability and quota scans, model-authored HANDOFF parser, fithandoff corrections, hop/visited limits, team-plan and team-start, task/role progress and user-visible review card. - Closure path: base A: coordinator sees current request and available agents → selects next specialist/brief → host validates target, quota and hop bound → launches next model into the same team turn → completed work returns for coordinator synthesis. parent P: operator inspects the proposed team steps/roles → confirms start or selects agents directly → startteam executes selected commitments, returns results and gives operator stop/rework control. - Boundary reachability: auto handoff is a shipped default (autohandoff true), splithandoff and continuation call the actual model launch; the explicit reviewed plan path is offered by first-party team-plan/team-start actions and takes effect on real role runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent can select the next current work commitment in the autonomous configured mode, while the operator has a genuinely distinct authorization path in the explicit-team mode. Deterministic caps merely bound both decisions. - Whole-system current view: the coordinator is briefed on callable team roles, their available connector/quota, the current shared chat and prior outputs; the human sees the complete team plan and current task/agent statuses in the app. - Current-control decision scope: current division of requested work among available team members, next assignment and effort, task-step commitment and interruption, not a company-wide financial/resource allocation claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: the shipped build→review team shape can provide a distinct model reviewer with a host-read snippet of actual changed files, separate from the implementer&#39;s prose report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementer reports success without grounding it in actual changed content or a second critique. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the plan or default FRIDAY review stage chooses the review function; a model may give a critique, but the host constructs the limited separate artifact-evidence packet, and no mandatory independent review acceptance gate is shipped. - Decision owner: constructed reviewer role with model critical judgment, invoked only in selected plans; human controls whether that optional stage is included and acted on. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default FRIDAY review stage, separate child chat, previous-stage report packet and host ProjectBridge reads of approved changed-file excerpts. - Closure path: build specialist reports completion → host reads approved changed files from attached project and supplies excerpt alongside untrusted implementation report → distinct reviewer model critiques it → critique enters team results and final JARVIS presentation for the user / next current action. - Boundary reachability: startteam defines a real default FRIDAY reviewer after VISION; collect reads actual project paths and appends changed-file excerpts; the runner invokes the reviewer using its own connector, when enabled and configured. Not every dynamic or explicitly planned workflow elects review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer can judge content independently of the implementer&#39;s wording, but first-party code only constructs a bounded evidence packet and optional review; the reviewer does not automatically receive independent command execution in kind=review, so C is safer than a strong autonomous audit owner claim. - Claim being audited: code implementation and claimed changed-file result from the prior build stage. - Ordinary reporting path: model-authored stage output saved as a team task result, then passed to downstream roles. - Complementary access path: host ProjectBridge.path(...).readtext() for approved changed-file requests, packaged as Changed file excerpts to the next review stage. - Independence boundary: separate model agent with actual file excerpts not supplied by model prose; however excerpts are partial, only for recorded approved requests, and reviewer has no independent project tool rights in this review stage. - Who acts on findings: reviewer returns concrete failure/missing checks through team presentation to the user; no automatic veto or remediation is claimed. - Evidence: default review stage and excerpt construction; review role prompt; bounded project file access. - Basis: constructor-specific alternate evidence path; no guarantee of mandatory audit or outcome correctness. - Confidence: medium-low due optional stage and incomplete raw-artifact coverage. - Caveats: the default team plan omits unavailable roles and a model-generated plan may omit reviewer entirely; source snippets are the same worktree the implementer can change, not an isolated verifier workspace.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environmental-sensing and capability-adaptation loop is closed at the installed team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future model/provider or external market changes are not transformed into a selected capability change by a shipped S4 decision process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: research specialist answers current tasks, while operator adjusts config; there is no routine future-option evaluation and adopted update path. - Decision owner: not supplied as a distinct adaptation authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search/research role, quotas, scheduled chat tasks, local role settings. - Closure path: task-local research may feed immediate work, but no exterior/future intelligence → alternative adaptation → approved capability revision loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live web search and code building within a user brief are S1; a scheduled task or quota warning alone is not future-oriented organization adaptation. - Evidence: research and code routing; team planner; scheduler. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human can ask a research→build sequence to improve the software, but that does not create a first-party enduring S4 procedure at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate-identity or policy-conflict decision transaction is closed through a legitimate internal parent authority and return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting purpose/ultimate-policy demands are not represented as a governable S5 issue by the application. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user grants OS/project permissions and configures agents, but no built-in exceptional identity/policy adjudicator is invoked and returned. - Decision owner: external Mac user and OS entitlement authorities for ordinary permissions, not a first-party S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-chat permission tiers, explicit risky-command approvals, prohibited app/action checks, selected role charters and user settings. - Closure path: user approves a requested tool action and it executes or is refused; that is local operation permission, not identity issue → final legitimate policy decision → governed future activity under a new policy resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard safety rails and permission consent are fixed constraints for S1/S3 operation; they are not a viable organization&#39;s policy/identity decision right. - Evidence: README permission and safety rails; permission mode and work decision routing; project bridge. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this denies a first-party closure path, not the existence of legitimate human or OS-level authority outside the assessed recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/713e4282b33db357f31b0ce2894da2edac983ebd/assessments/ja/jarvis-ai-council.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 713e4282b33db357f31b0ce2894da2edac983ebd\">713e428 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:14:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Mohamed-jarboui/agent-runtime-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LoopSmith</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Mohamed-jarboui/agent-runtime-harness/tree/0c6c7b170bc553be38909800ceb5132e52299e52\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c6c7b170bc553be38909800ceb5132e52299e52\">0c6c7b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Context-sensitive, model-directed workspace analysis and report construction through tools, handoffs and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different project files, user objectives, tool responses, untrusted search results, coding/report findings, and reviewer feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose file-inspection/search operations, tool arguments, model-generated findings/draft, and subsequent work from prior observations in configured live provider mode. - Decision owner: Model actor selected by AgentRuntime for the installed analyst role; first-party runtime owns constraints and tool execution, not the substantive task choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed tool registry, provider resolution, JSON/tool validation, session/SQLite state, bounded loop, memory, permissions, approval and safe workspace tools. - Closure path: Goal → model agent receives system/context/tools → chosen tool/delegation or answer → stored tool result/error → updated model context → further choice or terminal workflow result. - Boundary reachability: CLI registers analyst roles and workflows; documented --provider option chooses configured live Anthropic/OpenAI-compatible provider. Default mock mode is explicitly labelled deterministic and not counted as autonomous S1 by itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Live LLM selects meaningful investigation and writing actions; disabling the model leaves only scripted mock output and deterministic graph/tool enforcement. - Evidence: agent runtime loop, analyst role tools, CLI provider option. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party live-provider mode. - Confidence: High for model/tool decision wiring; actual model behavior/quality not exercised. - Caveats: Live credentials/model endpoint required; offline demo and evaluation suite cannot be substituted for live S1 evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No specific operational-unit interference attenuation relation with its own feedback is established in the shipped workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist steps may depend on previous outputs; concurrent/conflicting independent S1 behavior and a correction loop for their interaction are not demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinator role can delegate research and hand off drafting, while graph engine follows author-defined routes; neither detects interaction-generated peer oscillation. - Decision owner: Model coordinator for task delegation; developer-authored graph/deterministic runtime for ordered transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Allow-listed agent.delegate and agent.handoff, bounded depth/count, workflow contexts and conditional transitions. - Closure path: Coordinator → specialist result → next step, or workflow state → fixed branch; no peer disturbance observation → S2-specific attenuation decision → revised peer commitments loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Role routing, delegation and deterministic sequence are organizational plumbing, not proof of a distinct S2 regulatory function. - Evidence: meta-tools/handoffs, coordinator role, workflow. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A developer may compose multiple competing S1 teams with custom coordination, outside this reviewed installed mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct discretionary whole-system present-control authority over a portfolio of operational commitments is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Excessive tool calls, unsafe writes, retries, failed rubric checks and pending approvals; these are bounded by limits/gates within one task workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer authors workflow transitions and caps; reviewer judges the report as a specific QA result; human approves individual writes, but no owner revises multiple S1 resource/priority commitments from a current whole-system view. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for envelope, runtime for deterministic steps and human for write permission; no separate cross-operational S3 manager in standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent workflow checkpoints, maxSteps/maxVisits, permission engine, approval state and structured reviewer verdict route. - Closure path: Draft → verification/review → revise/publish or human gate; this is local artifact acceptance, not whole-operating-system resource/commitment balancing. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LoopSmith may be a reusable control-plane foundation, but it does not package a whole-portfolio S3 actor in the reviewed workflows; generic graph transitions alone do not count. - Evidence: workflow graph, workflow engine, permission defaults. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Higher-level orchestration/portfolio policy may be built by a customer from provided interfaces; no shipped specific owner/closure is established here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Evidence-facing independent report review, distinct from the report writer and fixed structural validators, with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Generated reports can omit findings, cite nonexistent file lines or inaccurately describe workspace evidence while passing basic formatting checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated reviewer agent returns approved/needschanges, identified issues and explicit checkedEvidence judgments after spot-checking cited source with read-only tool. - Decision owner: Separate reviewer model role in shipped analyst workflow; the graph engine routes its verdict and the executor addresses findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer tools:[&#39;workspace.read&#39;], permissions:{write:&#39;deny&#39;}, model instruction to inspect cited file/line, typed ReviewSchema and deterministic pre-review verifyReport structure checks. - Closure path: Researcher findings → executor draft → deterministic structure/coverage check → distinct reviewer reads cited source and renders verdict → failed verdict routes to draft revision with reviewer feedback → re-review or human escalation on visit cap. - Claim being audited: Draft technical improvement report accurately and completely presents workspace findings with valid citations and safe content. - Ordinary reporting path: Researcher model findings and executor model draft placed in workflow state; writer&#39;s own output is the completion claim. - Complementary access path: Reviewer has separate workspace.read access to actual workspace files and a required cited-line spot check, with structured checkedEvidence results; deterministic verifier supplies additional independently computed format checks. - Independence boundary: Named reviewer model role distinct from executor and researcher, its permissions deny file writes and it can directly inspect original code. It shares configured provider infrastructure and is not an external independent auditor; structural independence is at role/readonly-tool boundary. - Who acts on findings: Conditional workflow routes non-approval back to executor draft, inserting prior reviewer feedback; after visit limit, it asks a human whether to publish despite unresolved issues. - Boundary reachability: apps/cli/src/bootstrap.ts registers analystAgents/analystWorkflows; workspace-analyst is advertised in the README and has the review/return path first-party installed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer makes a discretionary evidence-verification judgment separate from writer&#39;s normal self-report; the code rule alone is not credited as S3. - Evidence: reviewer role/permissions, review and revision closure, installed bootstrap. - Basis: structural explicit first-party review closure. - Confidence: High for shipped definitions; moderate for live-model behavior because standard demo is deterministic. - Caveats: A single spot check is bounded evidence, not exhaustive verification. Structural checks and external provider performance cannot replace actual independent model review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No closed future-environment sensing and strategic adaptation loop changing the harness&#39;s future organizational capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Historical memory and observations are used to answer the current task; provider fallback handles present availability rather than long-term adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/operator changes prompts, agents, workflows and provider config; model planner selects current investigation steps only. - Decision owner: Developer/operator for prospective capability changes, task agents for current workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent memory, context compaction, configurable workflow templates, model/provider registry, evaluation CLI. - Closure path: Stored observations → current task context or test report; no external-future model → adaptation choice → capability-configuration feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistence, task planning and model fallback are not sufficient for future-facing organizational intelligence. - Evidence: context management, planner instructions, CLI evaluation. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External builders could use benchmarks and workflow reports to guide future strategy, but that is not a first-party installed feedback path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-purpose or identity dispute arbitration beyond per-operation permission rules and human action consent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tool arguments, writes, external MCP calls, destructive execution and untrusted input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures policies and human approves particular side effects; no packaged S5 actor decides durable system purpose or constitutional boundaries. - Decision owner: External operator/human for static permission config/individual approval, deterministic policy engine for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Allow/deny/require-approval categories, persistent approvals, workspace sandbox and secret redaction. - Closure path: Tool request → permission check or human approval → execute/deny/resume, not identity-level dispute → legitimate ultimate authority decision → enduring organizational mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model cannot override tool policy, and approvals are narrow operational gates, not ultimate policy identity choices. - Evidence: permission engine, human approvals, README boundaries. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User&#39;s external organization may have its own S5 beyond this library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7eb41a76f082be5690ca12269cc1eec41aa5a528/assessments/lo/loopsmith.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7eb41a76f082be5690ca12269cc1eec41aa5a528\">7eb41a7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:00:30+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rubin-johnson/maestro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maestro</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rubin-johnson/maestro/tree/0589ff3b2b0d25baabff196a1dd64c2147048b8e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0589ff3b2b0d25baabff196a1dd64c2147048b8e\">0589ff3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Code-changing task agents implement specified work units and respond to source/tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Requirements, repository state, build/test failures, model response and review-requested revisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Implementation CLI-backed model selects coding steps and edits; revision requests lead to a new model-driven implementation attempt. - Decision owner: Launched model-driven implementer for local coding decisions, installed into Maestro&#39;s first-party work-unit execution route. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tmux isolation, explicit work-unit spec/DoD, task limits, SQLite persistence, CLI backends and escalation. - Closure path: User work unit→Maestro spawns implementer→model edits repo files→tests/review results→model revision or final accepted code. - Boundary reachability: execute CLI and orchestrator instantiate real tmux coding agents with configured backends, not just mock or planned actors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model actor exercises task-level file-change choice; Maestro&#39;s fixed phase machine supplies gates, not coding autonomy. External vendor CLI internals are not separately credited. - Evidence: orchestrator, tmux manager, CLI. - Basis: Shipped runnable model-directed coding process with corrective feedback into implementation. - Confidence: Medium-high for first-party route; provider runtime effectiveness not independently replayed. - Caveats: Actual model steps run in separately installed CLI products; first-party evidence is their orchestration contract and returned status, not vendor-harness architecture.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate agent-owned regulation of specifically observed contention between parallel S1 implementation units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dependency readiness, parallel-file collisions and worker resource contention can occur across work units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: DAG dependency checks and maxconcurrent=4 semaphore select ready waves, but are deterministic ordering, not balancing of detected competing goals. - Decision owner: Operator for work-unit DAG; Python scheduler for static ordering and concurrency gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dependency graph, parallel executor, work-unit status, file scope checks and tmux windows. - Closure path: Predeclared dependency→ready/blocked status; parallel work-unit results→output records, not peer contention evidence→autonomous S2 arbitration→returned adjustments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A queue, DAG and semaphore are supporting coordination machinery but no distinct real-time S2 balancing authority is installed. - Evidence: workflow executor, parallel executor. - Basis: Bounded absence despite distinct simultaneous work units. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator can encode dependencies to avoid conflict; not a self-sensing inter-S1 balancing loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No model/agent-owned discretionary whole-system management of current operating commitments and resources beyond the authored work-unit sequence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed unit, bad test result, model timeout, context exhaustion and blocked dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fixed phase gates and escalation ladder retry/fail/advance per unit; code selects ready waves. Model-assisted decomposition serves task shaping, not a portfolio manager with authority over organizational tradeoffs. - Decision owner: User defines work-unit specs/priority/dependencies; deterministic orchestrator follows configured rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validator gates, model-tier escalation, retry budget, SQLite status, DAG and GitOps. - Closure path: Unit failure→preset retry/tier escalation; DAG dependency blocking→recorded status. No autonomous whole-portfolio capacity/priority/commitment renegotiation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforceable resource and quality gates are not an independent S3 deciding strategic current tradeoffs; reviewers judge claims, they do not manage fleet priorities. - Evidence: phase and escalation machine, escalation rules, decomposition. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing source-backed execution governance against S3 discretion threshold. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A future user/controller could wrap Maestro in a portfolio S3, but the standard execution mode owns only per-unit rule machinery.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Bounded independent code/spec reality check of implementation claims with a returned review verdict and enforced rework. - Claim being audited: Implementer asserts its work unit meets the original spec and Definition of Done and presents changed files/output. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer process completion text, status, staged changes and routine command/test output feed the orchestrator. - Complementary access path: Fresh cross-model reviewer agents receive the original spec/DoD and are asked to inspect actual code files and Git diff independently; deterministic validator directly executes source/test checks rather than trust self-report. - Independence boundary: Reviewer runs in a distinct tmux agent session and model type selected to avoid collision with implementer, but uses the same local repo and is orchestrated by Maestro; no external institution or externally verified truth guarantee. - Who acts on findings: Orchestrator parses NEEDSREVISION and validation failure, blocks commit and re-enters implementation or escalates model tier; positive evidence can advance to commit. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Hallucinated completion, incomplete DoD, out-of-scope file changes, security/code-quality defects and misleading model claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer may reject implementation with file:line evidence, while independent validator can fail executable checks; orchestrator enforces corrective loop. - Decision owner: Distinct reviewer model owns evidence-based audit judgment; deterministic validator owns command-based test gate; orchestrator routes returned finding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fresh reviewer prompt, different agent type, direct source access, git diff, file-scope manifest, test execution, verdict parser and retry/escalation. - Closure path: Implementer completion claim→fresh independent reviewer reads code/spec (plus test execution/diff)→verdict→orchestrator rejects/requests revision or accepts/commits→new implementation attempt. - Boundary reachability: The shipped review phase spawns its own tmux reviewer, sends actual work-unit spec/diff/file-scope instructions and persists result; source tests cover review/revision behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer challenges original operational claims with a separately acquired direct file view, not just repackaged implementer prose. The static accept/deny state machine is an enforcement channel. - Evidence: review creation and non-colliding model selection, reviewer instruction, orchestrator revision path, validators. - Basis: Source-traced alternative direct-code reality access, distinct judgment owner and closed returned correction in normal work-unit mode. - Confidence: Medium-high for implemented bounded audit route. - Caveats: Reviewers can err, share upstream tool vulnerabilities or be manipulated by inputs; using a different model does not alone prove independence, and this is not certified external audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous future-environment sensing and strategic organizational change loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing model quality/prices, future source architecture, toolchain updates and novel task types. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators configure ladder/backend; predictive risk, preflight and dynamic decomposition serve a current task&#39;s execution, not future organizational strategy. - Decision owner: External developer/operator for long-horizon capability changes; per-unit implementer/reviewer for present work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider ladder, cost tier, prediction, knowledge-base roadmap and context-budget monitoring. - Closure path: Failure→current retry with a stronger model; offline future development remains manual. No external scanning→future options→policy/operational mandate adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Changing model tier in response to immediate test failures is operational retry, not adaptive prospective environmental intelligence. - Evidence: model escalation, prediction, orchestrator. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User can ask Maestro to implement a future plan, which is task-level S1 rather than its organizational S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate identity-policy governing body or returned parent-assisted mandate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe code changes, permission policy, task scope and model/provider credentials. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User authors work spec/DoD, mode, policies and credentials; validator/reviewer enforce work acceptance rather than determine organizational identity. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for legitimate policy; deterministic gates and reviewer for local task acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manifest scope, approval mode, preflight DoD quality, explicit backend configuration and commit gate. - Closure path: User spec/config→bounded operational checks; no identity-level conflict→legitimate charter deliberation→binding mandate into S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Security and acceptance criteria around a work unit do not constitute self-directed ultimate governance. - Evidence: preflight, review, config. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A human owner may be S5 of a deploying team, but not installed as an autonomous or parent-governed Maestro role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/466587e24fbc9020d697440ea720db9274be8207/assessments/ru/rubin-johnson-maestro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 466587e24fbc9020d697440ea720db9274be8207\">466587e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:29:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BlackJaxDev/local-agent-broker\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Local Agent Broker</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BlackJaxDev/local-agent-broker/tree/86b09380e3393126d6c540787a7576a61d21565b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86b09380e3393126d6c540787a7576a61d21565b\">86b0938 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-controlled workers transform bounded objectives into answers, tool-mediated effects, or code-change proposals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain objectives, changing tool results, failed or denied calls, and code-edit details in authorized source files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The provider model selects its response, tool calls and next steps within broker-enforced limits; swarm leaves choose concrete source replacements. - Decision owner: External API model invoked as the active autonomous actor through the first-party broker loop; in swarm mode the per-phase root/leaf models choose plan and edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP transport, exact-model check, prompt construction, tool registry, immutable snapshots, budgets, provider retries, session policy and parallelism semaphores. - Closure path: Caller starts a run → model chooses answer/tool action → broker executes allowed tool → result returns on the next model turn → completed answer; in swarm mode leaf proposals return through review and caller-controlled apply. - Boundary reachability: startagentrun invokes AgentRunRegistry.Start and AgentOrchestrator.RunAsync; optional swarm routes through RunSwarmAsync/RunNodeAsync in shipped production code, not a test-only agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides task content/actions. Hard tool caps and file permissions only constrain or reject those decisions, rather than replacing their discretion. - Evidence: MCP tool implementation, agent turn/tool feedback, swarm leaves. - Basis: Structural first-party model-execution and feedback path. - Confidence: High for code wiring; medium for real provider execution because no billed run was repeated here. - Caveats: A standalone broker invocation still needs explicit provider credentials and client authorization; provider cognition is not itself first-party broker implementation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No established adaptive inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation with returned behavioral adjustment in the shipped modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel child agents can propose overlapping edits to a shared source file; separately queued workers can contend for concurrency capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The caller fixes allowed paths and limits; deterministic merger rejects overlapping source edits rather than negotiating a revised peer coordination response. - Decision owner: Caller for path allocations/limits; root model for initial task decomposition; runtime for conflict validation and static semaphore enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-child path assignment, immutable base hashes, overlap rejection, provider-slot and swarm-slot semaphores. - Closure path: Parallel child edits → conflict checker either combines non-overlapping patches or fails the proposal; no installed conflict-resolution decision goes back into a new coordinated child round. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct S1 leaves and a real possible edit collision are present, but a rejection gate and initial delegation are not an S2-specific feedback regulator changing subsequent inter-S1 behavior. - Evidence: plan constraints, overlap detection, parallel execution. - Basis: Bounded negative functional assessment despite a structurally identifiable disturbance. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External callers could retry with changed scopes; such a coordination actor/loop is not installed in the broker itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous whole-system current-control authority over a portfolio of active work and shared commitments beyond bounded delegation rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, spending/turn/tool budgets, child-run admission, timeout and cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller fixes model, task, tool permissions and budgets; swarm root generates a one-time file-scoped task plan, while the runtime enforces ceilings and aborts on failure. - Decision owner: Caller/operator owns shared limits and resource policy; model&#39;s root plan owns current task decomposition, not ongoing organizational capacity or commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRunRegistry, global concurrency, AgentRunBudget, per-phase token reservation, node admission caps, failure propagation and status registry. - Closure path: Preset limits → runtime admission/scheduling/termination; the shipped loop lacks a whole-run current-state appraisal feeding discretionary reallocation of operational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model task splitting is not evidenced whole-system current-resource bargaining or authoritative intervention; semaphores/budgets enforce caller choices. - Evidence: run registry, swarm plan, limits and failure propagation. - Basis: Bounded negative after testing root-orchestrator and runtime-manager paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Host clients may provide higher-recursion fleet S3; their decisions are outside this broker&#39;s boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-owned source-backed review of child agent code-change claims before approval or application. - Claim being audited: Each leaf&#39;s proposed replacement is a valid contribution to the parent objective and success criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Leaf model emits JSON path, basesha256, oldtext and newtext; runner collects child artifacts. - Complementary access path: A fresh parent review API request receives the unchanged child artifacts and broker-captured immutable source snapshots and original objective, not only the leaf&#39;s completion assertion. The deterministic merger separately validates base hashes/overlap. - Independence boundary: The review runs as a distinct provider phase after leaves finish, without their continuation or permission to modify their artifacts; the child cannot mark its own edits approved. The same exact model/provider family may be used and all inputs are broker-supplied snapshots, so this is bounded internal complementary inspection, not cross-model or external institutional audit. - Who acts on findings: RunNodeAsync rejects the entire swarm when the parent model returns approved=false; only positive review returns proposals to the run registry for caller review or explicit Windows application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but faulty code replacements, mismatch to objective and misleading leaf success claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate parent reviewer model decides approved and summary after examining source and unchanged proposed edits; deterministic orchestrator enforces that verdict. - Decision owner: Review-phase provider model makes the substantive accept/reject judgment; runner parser and fail path are supporting enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable source snapshot capture, isolated phase request with no tools, review-specific instructions, JSON parse, merge collision check and no-change-on-rejection guard. - Closure path: Leaf proposals → fresh model review with original source/artifacts → verdict → rejection terminates with no approved changes, or approval exposes the proposal for caller acceptance and possible readback-checked application. - Boundary reachability: Optional production swarm uses RunNodeAsync for every orchestrator node, creates a distinct CreatePhaseRequest with context-file snapshots for review, and RunSwarmAsync exposes only approved changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer independently judges a source-backed child claim rather than trusting its self-report; the deterministic parser does not supply the substantive review decision. - Evidence: review request and input wiring, review verdict closure, provider snapshot input, rejection test. - Basis: Structural distinct audit-judgment call with source evidence and returned corrective veto. - Confidence: Medium: model decision and wiring established in source; actual provider-backed judgments not replayed. - Caveats: Same model/provider can produce correlated errors; review sees captured source and proposals, not independently sampled live post-apply code. This is not a general-purpose S3 of all broker runs.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external/future environmental sensing, option formation, and adopted adaptation loop for the broker itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing provider options, future model costs/capabilities, repository evolution and new threats. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller selects actual model/configuration; keyword-based route advisor can recommend a model for the current objective, not revise future organizational capability. - Decision owner: Human caller/developer for model/policy upgrades; deterministic AgentRouteAdvisor for present-task recommendation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Exact-model catalog, task keyword classifier, provider error/retry diagnostics and operator settings. - Closure path: Current objective → static model recommendation → caller choice for this run; no external sensing→prospective options→capability adoption path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task difficulty routing and provider retries respond to current execution, not environmental foresight or adopted future change. - Evidence: route advisor, broker config, run/model validation. - Basis: Bounded negative after route/adaptation candidates were inspected. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A user may delegate future-looking research to an individual worker; that is operational S1 output, not an established broker-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy deliberation and legitimate return-to-runtime decision loop at this broker recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task authority, source-file permissions, credential exposure, destructive editor commands and uncontrolled billed model execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller and configuration supply boundaries; deterministic validators reject unauthorized requests. There is no shipped identity/charter question escalated to and resolved by an ultimate authority with a returned mandate. - Decision owner: External MCP caller/operator for task authorization and security policy; program validators enforce rather than govern its content. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit workspace/allowed paths, tool annotations and allowlist, destructive/mutation flags, exact model requirement, budget checks and secret-path denial. - Closure path: Operator-supplied scope → fixed request guards → permitted/denied run; no identity-level issue→legitimate parent authority→binding policy decision→changed subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordinary permissions and operator review of a patch are run-level controls, not a first-party S5 organization or parent-governed identity loop. - Evidence: protocol policy, repository path policy, run admission. - Basis: Bounded negative for the standard-distribution authority path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The parent organization using the broker may have an S5; its external decisions are not part of this local harness&#39;s evidenced operating structure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a8b7d8aa1af85fabe5957742ed23c9358007621d/assessments/lo/local-agent-broker.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a8b7d8aa1af85fabe5957742ed23c9358007621d\">a8b7d8a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:29:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-RUA/uni-robot-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Uni Robot Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-RUA/uni-robot-agent/tree/2590ab48b7c24a72123597f2d5b04c9524e4329c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2590ab48b7c24a72123597f2d5b04c9524e4329c\">2590ab4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous task execution through repeated robot observation, tool/action choice and resulting environmental feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object poses, grasp failures, occluded views, tool errors, unavailable resources and differences between expected and measured effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coding-agent model chooses which available robot tool to call, with what arguments, whether to inspect/recover and when to request finish. - Decision owner: model-backed Codex/Claude task agent in the shipped run/TUI session; the SDK and provider implement its inference, while the first-party harness wires its decisions to robots. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party MCP/Claude tool adapters, shared Episode, resource executor, snapshots, action-size bounds, state-version checks, receipts, time/step budgets and structured feedback. - Closure path: task instruction and scene → model chooses a registered tool → executor/environment performs or rejects a bounded action → result, image and updated state return into the agent&#39;s next decision → task continues or finishes. - Boundary reachability: session.py actually selects and invokes a packaged Codex/Claude adapter with registered tools; no separate operator-authored control program is necessary after starting the supported model-backed episode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent has genuine contextual discretion among actions and recovery steps; deterministic guards and robot services restrict or carry out those choices but cannot replace the agent&#39;s task decisions. - Evidence: main run cycle, Claude SDK run and tool interaction, Codex SDK run, tool/episode flow. - Basis: structural code and explicit shipped run documentation; no live model-provider run was claimed in this review. - Confidence: high for the supported agent-to-tool/feedback decision path; physical task success remains unverified here. - Caveats: the model/SDK must be installed and configured; such dependencies do not make the first-party integrated agent path merely a non-agentic tool library.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 conflict-attenuation relation was established at the declared episode recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent motion/tool calls can conflict over an arm, an episode or its state version, but the reviewed paths serialize tools of one task-agent&#39;s execution rather than reconcile distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured FIFO resource placement and immutable-snapshot rules govern tool execution; no first-party autonomous inter-S1 adjustment choice was found. - Decision owner: deterministic scheduler/developer configuration, not an S2 agent decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolExecutionPolicy, per-resource exclusive queues, background job handles, global-serial option and SDK subagent support. - Closure path: incompatible calls wait for a resource and receive completion feedback, but this is intra-episode execution safety, not a separately evidenced cross-S1 coordination conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SDK-native subagents and two physical arms do not by themselves establish two organizational S1 units with their own outcomes and a specific reciprocal disturbance. - Evidence: scheduling and resource semantics, scheduler, agent adapter boundaries. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of shipped robot-tool and agent delegation modes. - Confidence: medium-high at the episode boundary. - Caveats: a separate multi-robot or multi-agent deployment might establish S2, but that composition is not inferred from SDK capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-system current-control owner over multiple S1 commitments/resources was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: running time, token/tool limits, physical safety, stale observations and missed action acknowledgements are regulated locally within an episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator/developer selects budgets and modes; runtime rejects/queues/cancels actions according to those constraints, while the task agent chooses local work. - Decision owner: configuration/episode executor, not a distinct metasystem owner with current whole-system bargaining discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: timeout, step limit, resource queues, receipts, cancellation and TUI/CLI status. - Closure path: bounded local execution and failures feed the same agent&#39;s continuation; no portfolio-level current resource decision returns to several operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: calling the executor a controller does not establish S3; it does not independently allocate/revise commitments across an operational whole. - Evidence: session lifecycle, scheduler, architecture. - Basis: bounded absence review of implemented execution-control and user modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: parent human instructions and operational safety guards are real, but neither automatically closes S3 at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: an opt-in independently prompted model checks a subordinate VLA&#39;s proposed motor-action chunk against current raw observations, measured state and expected task progress, then can require a correction or stop before execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the Pi action model can propose an unsafe, geometrically misaligned or already-completed prefix despite producing a normal-looking trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer model selects student, edit, eef or stop, number of steps, reason and continuation after judging the proposal and physical evidence; ActorRunner uses this decision to execute, modify or withhold the proposed chunk. - Decision owner: the separately invoked VLA reviewer model in supported RoboDojo Hybrid reviewer-enabled mode; deterministic validation/bounds constrain, but do not choose, its evaluative verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh camera capture, measured state and FK proposal, separate reviewer prompt/Responses call, structured validator, chunk archive, bounded correction commands and execution receipts. - Closure path: Pi proposes action prefix → independent reviewer receives proposal plus raw/derived observation → reviewer returns corrective or stop judgment → ActorRunner changes executed commands or halts → actual feedback and previous review enter the next chunk or higher-level task agent. - Boundary reachability: the shipped --action-profile hybrid path constructs ChunkReviewer by default when binding supports it, requires an explicit Responses-compatible endpoint, and calls its decide before any chunk execution. It is not a paper-only evaluator or an uninstalled CI process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer can counter the subordinate actor&#39;s suggestion with independent discretionary evidence assessment and corrective action; it is not simply a deterministic success flag. This is mode-specific and cannot be generalized to Direct mode or to unconfigured endpoints. - Claim being audited: the proposed VLA action is aligned with current physical observations, task intent and local progress, and its continuation is warranted. - Ordinary reporting path: VLA proposal/trajectory and main task agent&#39;s instruction/task-progress report. - Complementary access path: separately prompted reviewer model sees raw head/wrist camera images, measured pose/FK checks, native feedback and prior reviewer context before command commitment. - Independence boundary: the VLA does not choose or edit the review verdict; the reviewer call has distinct prompt/context and can veto/correct, although the same provider/model family may be configured and both remain inside one product&#39;s action path. - Who acts on findings: ActorRunner applies stop, altered command prefixes or continued student actions; the main agent receives final tool feedback for its next choice. - Evidence: reviewer decision model, source-grounded reviewer prompt, execution after verdict, reviewer enablement. - Basis: structural first-party review/judgment and actuation-return wiring; no separate provider E2E was performed. - Confidence: medium for materially complementary access, high for the executable verdict-to-command decision path. - Caveats: no assurance of model reliability or institutionally independent review follows. Hybrid requires a model endpoint, action checkpoint and integration. This state credits the installed optional reviewer path, not routine task success checks or offline evaluation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party outside-and-prospective option-selection conversation returning to current whole-episode capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environments, robot geometries and repeated failures could motivate improved skills or action models. - Decisive decision or feedback right: writeskill optionally lets the task agent store an observed lesson in an operator-configured external library; developers choose model/integration updates. Neither shows a separate established future-environment intelligence and S3 adaptation loop. - Decision owner: task agent owns local lesson text when enabled; operator/developer chooses whether/how to reuse broader configuration and model improvements. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: opt-in skill-writing, read/list skill tools, prompt guidance that requires evidence and narrow transfer, saved traces and optional SFT export. - Closure path: a verified task lesson may be stored for a later run, but review found no first-party prospective external-option decision with a defined whole-system capability adoption/return path; ordinary memory reuse is not sufficient. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: experience preservation can alter later task context, but it is not by itself S4&#39;s external-and-then intelligence conversation. - Evidence: skill-writing implementation, lesson instructions, architecture&#39;s optional skill mode. - Basis: bounded negative review distinguishing operational experience from prospective environmental adaptation. - Confidence: medium; externally oriented future modes may be added later. - Caveats: this does not deny useful cross-run skill improvement or possible future S4 constructor work.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate runtime identity/ultimate-policy issue-to-authority-to-operation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over robot-task safety, data/model choice, user purpose and deployment policy could raise higher-level identity questions, but these remain outside the reviewed episode governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a user chooses task/mode/constraints; first-party runtime enforces tool permissions and safety bounds, without a distinct S5 deliberation/return path. - Decision owner: external human/project authority for product identity; task-local model/guard machinery does not own ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI configuration, model/tool permissions, tool restrictions, explicit finish acknowledgement and environment safety state. - Closure path: configuration constrains next actions and safety checks reject disallowed movement; no observed identity dispute is escalated to a legitimate parent and returned as a revised binding ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human task instructions and deterministic safety policy do not become autonomous or parent-governed S5 merely because they are authoritative for one episode. - Evidence: configuration, run structure, episode and tools. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of runtime control, human interaction and governance scope. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared episode boundary. - Caveats: the external robotics organization may possess its own S5; it is not part of this first-party deployed harness by repository adjacency.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b1c0dda8f43c21cc9cbab1d1db5c651a537454d4/assessments/un/uni-robot-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b1c0dda8f43c21cc9cbab1d1db5c651a537454d4\">b1c0dda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:07:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phuryn/mini-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mini Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phuryn/mini-harness/tree/7ae9fca8221c55bd3c38697a61075357e1a94c26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ae9fca8221c55bd3c38697a61075357e1a94c26\">7ae9fca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided local work with iterative tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing files, shell command outputs, tool errors, user objectives and MCP responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses next tool and arguments after observations, can stop or delegate a bounded reading task. - Decision owner: Model executing in first-party Agent.turn/dispatch; installed harness executes selected calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LocalTools, approvals, MCP hub, append-only session, context compaction and goal mode. - Closure path: User/goal → model inference → selected local/MCP tool → real result → appended observation → another model choice or answer. - Boundary reachability: Supported CLI instantiates an actual model client and supplies tools; fake-model tests exercise the same loop, but paid-provider operation was not replayed here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model is able to choose an action and change it from observations; tool runtime and permissions do not own the choice. - Evidence: loop and dispatch, tools, session. - Basis: First-party model→tool→observation→redecision implementation and reachable CLI. - Confidence: High for code path; no paid-provider E2E claim. - Caveats: Provider behavior, external MCP cognition and human permission remain outside first-party ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No ongoing regulation of contention between multiple autonomous operational S1 units is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel demands or access conflict among distinct workers could arise, but task delegates a standalone reading request rather than balancing competing S1 commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Main agent may delegate; no independent conflict arbitration loop decides inter-worker priorities. - Decision owner: Main S1 model for delegation and operator for permission; none for a separate S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fresh-session task subagents, tool approval and session separation. - Closure path: Main model→child task→child text→main model; no discovered contention→S2 arbitration→corrected peer commitments path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequential or concurrent delegation is not itself feedback regulation between S1 units. - Evidence: subagent invocation, session. - Basis: Bounded absence review of installed delegate and tool gates. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User-built external MCP servers might coordinate their own agents outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No discretionary control of present resources, priorities and commitments across a whole set of autonomous S1 operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Goal turn/budget exhaustion, failed verification, tool risk and paused work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: /goal performs fixed turn/budget/status gates and deterministic pass-or-retry; users choose the verification command and budget. - Decision owner: Human operator owns scope/budgets; code enforces fixed conditions. Main model still owns task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: rungoal turn limit, spend meter, verify, permission approval and pause/resume. - Closure path: Claimed completion→verification/reviewer gate→accept or return disagreement to same S1; no discretionary cross-unit resources/commitment management. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong operational gating does not itself imply a separate S3 decision authority over an S1 portfolio. - Evidence: goal and verification, permission gate. - Basis: Bounded current-control absence despite a real goal-acceptance gate. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separately deployed fleet supervisor could add S3, but is not a first-party standard mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate fresh-context inspection of a worker&#39;s completion claim using additional operational evidence before accepting a goal. - Claim being audited: The main model declares that the current /goal objective is fully complete and supplies goaldone evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: The worker&#39;s tool observations and self-reported completion argument arrive through its existing session and goal claim. - Complementary access path: Fresh-context reviewer receives the objective and claim and can directly inspect current working-directory files using its own tool calls; optional shell verifier independently executes user-specified checks. - Independence boundary: Distinct reviewer session and tool calls but the same first-party code/provider client and host permission policy; reviewer is not a separately governed institution. - Who acts on findings: Agent.rungoal rejects unapproved completions, returns negative reviewer findings to worker and repeats until accepted or stopped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False self-reported completion, missing/incorrect local artifacts and a worker&#39;s biased summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer independently reads working-directory evidence and returns VERDICT: DONE or rejection; goal controller accepts only a positive verdict after optional independent shell verification. - Decision owner: Fresh reviewer model for the audit judgment; harness for deterministic acceptance and rework routing; operator for optional verify definition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: New reviewer session, actual LocalTools read/bash access, distinct review instructions, optional shell verify, literal verdict parser and rework prompt. - Closure path: Worker goaldone claim→independent shell check when configured→new reviewer reads workspace→review judgment→goal controller acceptance or discrepancy returned to worker→corrective next turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer receives the goal and claim but has a genuinely separate workspace observation path and can reject the claim, rather than merely grading its prose; the gate only enforces the decision. - Evidence: REVIEW, runsubagent, rungoal, verify, reviewer false-claim test. - Boundary reachability: Supported /goal invokes verify on goaldone; bundled tests cover rejection, retry and fresh reviewer tools, without requiring a hypothetical external plugin. - Basis: Source-traced complementary evidence access, separate judgment, explicit corrective return in supported /goal mode. - Confidence: Medium-high for installed bounded path, not independent external audit quality. - Caveats: Same provider/client and workspace permissions; reviewer may be wrong, can be influenced by claim text, and is triggered by worker&#39;s completion claim. Independence is complementary to the worker&#39;s ordinary report, not organizational or institutional independence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous prospective environment-scanning and strategy/adaptation cycle for the harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model availability, new skills and MCP sources, changing user needs and context size. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human configures external services and models; compaction summarizes current conversation and goal remains a current task. - Decision owner: Operator for future capability selection, present S1 for ordinary research within user request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skills, MCP discovery, model presets, compaction, session resume and subagent research. - Closure path: Updated config or task findings may change next operation; no future-environment forecast→strategic selection→adopted change in S3 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Compaction/memory and delegated investigation support present tasks without autonomous mandate to reconfigure future capacity. - Evidence: loop/model config, external service hooks. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Individual agent may discuss future scenarios as an S1 user task.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct ultimate identity-policy or parent-governed mandate cycle in the installed task harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission-sensitive requests, unsafe tool invocations and operator goal changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator approves/denies risky actions and sets goal; optional cheap-model permission judge recommends local allowances under a fixed gate. - Decision owner: External user and static permission rules; no independent legitimate S5 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Approval prompts, optional permission judge, goal entry and local policy/configuration. - Closure path: User/permission decision→tool allow/deny; no identity-level conflict→legitimate policy decision→returned organizational mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission vetting is a task action constraint, not constitutive identity governance. - Evidence: permissions, goal setup. - Basis: Bounded absence review, including model-assisted permission path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External organization or human can exercise S5 outside this first-party product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f4373c2a406cda6a71e08c5c68ac4810c7d980da/assessments/ph/phuryn-mini-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f4373c2a406cda6a71e08c5c68ac4810c7d980da\">f4373c2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:20:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genproof/QuickMaths\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">QuickMaths</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genproof/QuickMaths/tree/c2dbb59e13b3cce451b86e600345361b34e8c5ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2dbb59e13b3cce451b86e600345361b34e8c5ba\">c2dbb59 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Transform a mathematical problem or contracted proof lane into proof artifacts through successive agent choices and compiler observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open proof holes, library availability, compiler errors, failed tactics, quota limits and continued work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose mathematical steps, edits, searches and next proof tactics within the supplied problem/lane contract. - Decision owner: Model-driven proving agent in the concrete first-party prepared job/role composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned external Claude Code executes model rounds; first-party agentrunner, Landlock boundary, ./lean broker, snapshots, role briefs, budgets and continuation sustain them. - Closure path: Prepared job/brief launches the pinned CLI with workspace tools; ./lean returns build/search/inspect observations; later edits/rounds consume those observations and retained notes/artifacts. Official verification alone controls final acceptance. - Boundary reachability: controller prepares and starts the packaged runtime; feeder attaches actual role briefs to assignments. This credits the shipped proving composition, without borrowing unrelated Claude Code functions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-driven prover leaves supervision and compilation but no choice of proof construction or repair. - Evidence: fleet/server/controller.py; runtime/leaneval/jobs.py; runtime/leaneval/agentrunner.py; runtime/leaneval/worker.py; fleet/server/briefs/COMMON.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate conflicting interfaces and duplicate shared machinery among concurrent proof-lane S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A consumer needs a stronger producer statement, a lane refutes a shared assumption, or consumers independently re-derive incompatible shared machinery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Architect grants/refuses interface requests, revises producing/consuming contracts and preserves only still-valid finished lanes. - Decision owner: The model-driven ARCHITECT/REARCHITECT agent at the lane-program coordination boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lane write confinement, InterfaceRequests/refutation extraction, validated reductions/sanity statements, bounded rearchitecture, merged overlays and redispatch enforce and transport the chosen response. Fleet de-duplication is an additional deterministic path, not the agent owner. - Closure path: LANE writes a concrete request/refutation; feeder collects it and supplies findings to a later architect; architect chooses corrected statements; validated architecture becomes the new base and subsequent lane assignments use those contracts. - Boundary reachability: feeder updateprogram/programassignment wire the shipped role briefs and findings into actual model rounds, then replace active lane state after validation. This is a supported operating path, not contributor dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic code can detect/reforward a mismatch, but cannot choose the corrected mathematical interface or grant/refuse the request without the architect. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent producer and consumer LANE proof work cells, each with a separate proof outcome and directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Consumer requirements exceed producer contracts or a refuted interface invalidates dependent lanes; concrete request/refutation fields and source tests expose this collision. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared interface contracts, one shared-tool producer, request-to-architect revision and invalidation of dependent completed claims. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Corrected architecture replaces lane statements and subsequent dispatched/restarted work uses the new base; invalid consumers cannot remain done. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The return changes a specific producer–consumer interface conflict rather than merely assigning tasks or transporting results. - Evidence: fleet/server/briefs/roles/ARCHITECT.md; fleet/server/briefs/roles/LANE.md; fleet/server/briefs/roles/REARCHITECT.md; fleet/server/feeder.py; fleet/tests/testfeeder.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: Regulate current campaign commitments, placement, continuation, competing routes and scarce account/runner resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Quota-exhausted work alongside idle usable accounts, duplicated programs, stalled lineages and unsuitable placements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/revise fleet policies and current interventions: hold/release, stop, hand off, split, hedge or rearchitect on behalf of the campaign. - Decision owner: Base C: a developer must compose the discretionary autonomous campaign controller over the specific current-view/plan/inbox/operator interfaces. Parent P: the legitimate campaign experiment manager/operator owns those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Deterministic coordinator.plan/execute, feeder scheduling, inbox outcomes, locks, controlled STOP and quota gates enforce selected constraints; their fixed rules are not autonomous organizational judgment. - Closure path: Gathered fleet status and lane/claim evidence inform manager decisions; --apply/inbox and operator commands change holds, claims, seeds or program architecture; feeder readback and later assignments enact the decision. - Boundary reachability: Deployment docs explicitly expose fleet status, coordinator apply and leh op to the experiment manager. Unlike a generic kill switch, these are campaign-specific current-control interfaces. No automatically running agent manager is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: With the manager removed, configured rules still enforce preselected policy but do not independently revise campaign priorities. A possible supervised agent session does not establish autonomous ownership of the reserved manager decisions. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator gathers hosts, accounts, live lineages, passes, targets, programs and prior directive outcomes; manager reads campaign status plus residual/quota information. - Current-control decision scope: Cross-host resource/commitment intervention and campaign/program continuation/route decisions, rather than just one proof tool call. - Evidence: fleet/local/coordinator.py; fleet/server/feeder.py; fleet/docs/COORDINATOR-POLICY.md; fleet/operator/README.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md - Basis: structural with explicit documented parent procedure - Confidence: high for P and specific construction surfaces; no A claim - Caveats: C does not grade deterministic scheduling as agent-owned. Parent authority is local to the operated campaign, not the OSS contributor organization.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complement ordinary progress claims with direct proof-graph/statement inspection of a nonconverging program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported RESIDUALS estimates can shrink through renaming while actual holes remain; false/vacuous lane assumptions can invalidate apparent progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge mismatch between agent progress claims and direct Lean declaration/statement reality, then require corrective intervention. - Decision owner: A separately composed experiment-manager auditor; the first-party audit-specific procedure and evidence/control path are supplied, but autonomous auditor execution/authority is not automatically wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leh map builds the merged tree and extracts actual reached/sorry-dependent declarations/refutations; operator tools expose artifacts/notes and rearchitect/stop updates. Lean execution and JSON summaries are evidence machinery, not the audit-judgment owner. - Closure path: METHOD §9 requires periodic map-versus-RESIDUALS comparison; the manager stop-and-audit procedure inspects statement truth/source strength and routes findings into rearchitecture/hedges or corrected program briefs through leh op. The direct map is advisory until that actor acts. - Boundary reachability: The installed leh map and deployed operator tools are linked by the supported manager method. It is an intentionally exposed campaign-audit construction, distinct from repository AUDIT.md or mandatory official acceptance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The map exposes materially different facts, but does not itself decide or implement the audit judgment; the supervised manager/agent setup remains to be composed for autonomous closure. - Claim being audited: Reported progress/completion and validity/usefulness of lane statements. - Ordinary reporting path: NOTES RESIDUALS/HANDOFF, lane done state and feeder convergence summaries. - Complementary access path: Build the merged proof tree, walk actual Lean declaration reachability/sorry/refutation dependencies, inspect published proof and concrete statement counterexamples. - Independence boundary: Manager does not prove the lanes; direct graph/statement access is outside self-reported estimates. Official verifier alone is not treated as S3. - Who acts on findings: Experiment manager selects corrective stop/rearchitecture/hedge; operator tools return findings to architect and subsequent lane rounds. - Evidence: docs/METHOD.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; src/harness/map/build.py; src/harness/map/summary.py; fleet/operator/rearchitect.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: No autonomous always-on auditor is claimed, and this assessment did not run Lean graph extraction. Human-owned audit is not published as P in this Methodology.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Develop the campaign capability repertoire from external mathematical/library opportunities under experiment-manager direction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Upcoming proof ingredients absent from current workbench versus existing licensed formalizations, changed/problematic benchmark statements and future evaluation feasibility. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose external reusable sources or revised methods to bring into the proving capability, with human acceptance and rollout. - Decision owner: The legitimate campaign experiment manager; documentation explicitly says a person decides/applies adaptation. A supervised operator agent can assist without establishing autonomous adaptation authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager literature/web recon and evolve reports support option development; bank add pins/license-checks sources, deploy distributes accepted briefs/policies, and worker search exposes admitted bank entries. - Closure path: Manager compares current convergence/capability gaps with published sources and future campaign needs, chooses reuse/admission or method change, adds pinned formalizations to the durable workbench bank or commits/redeploys approved changes; later architects/lanes search/use them and report reuse. Subsequent reports compare effects. - Boundary reachability: METHOD §§6,8,10 and manager instructions explicitly connect external recon to the bank/briefs; bank.py and worker.py implement the returned capability change. Evolve alone, being retrospective internal analysis, would not establish S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model proof search and deterministic proposal reports cannot independently approve/admit campaign adaptation; the documented manager remains the decisive parent. - External distinction: Relevant published mathematics/formalizations, benchmark issue information and organizer resource envelope beyond the current proof workspace. - Future / prospective distinction: Whether forthcoming campaign/lane work should reuse new ingredients or change method rather than repeat unsupported construction; durable bank admission changes the repertoire of later rounds. - Adaptation option generated: Acquire a licensed pinned formalization as a reusable tool source, revise role briefs/gates, or retain the current capability after comparison. - Path back into current capability / S3: Manager bank admission makes new sources searchable by the running runtime; accepted policy/brief changes are tested/deployed and used by later fleet assignments. - Evidence: docs/METHOD.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; docs/EVOLVE.md; runtime/tools/bank.py; runtime/leaneval/worker.py; fleet/server/briefs/roles/ARCHITECT.md - Basis: explicit documented operating procedure plus structural return path - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: A search for a present proof ingredient alone is S1; credit is limited to the documented manager capability-acquisition/rollout loop. No campaign transcript, measured adaptation benefit or autonomous Director is claimed.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Standing safety/source/acceptance rules and a person deciding submission/publication constrain the campaign; no runtime identity/ultimate-policy issue process with legitimate authoritative return is supplied. Experiment-manager current control and approved method updates do not automatically become S5. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static source/credential/acceptance constraints, profile.toml licensing rules, reviewed evolution commits and publish/trust decisions; none establishes a distinct identity-policy deliberation/escalation loop. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; docs/EVOLVE.md; docs/CONFIGURATION.md; runtime/leaneval/resources/profile.toml; fleet/local/publish.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dc4fff62cbaa1faa7b2e604db7a71ece9e95521b/assessments/qu/quickmaths.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dc4fff62cbaa1faa7b2e604db7a71ece9e95521b\">dc4fff6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:50:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NorthCUA</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA/tree/fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\">fb5f9e2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out one user computer-use task through observed Desktop/Web state and model-chosen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown application screens, changed DOM/state, unexpected tool responses, context growth, unsatisfied task conditions and execution interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next computer-use/tool action or finish based on observed state and returned tool results. - Decision owner: Configured agent model operating in the first-party Desktop/Web round executor; external inference service supplies model computation but does not implement the NorthCUA task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NexAU-compatible model adapter, typed tool contracts, context/image window, working notes, action backend, controller/device bridge, step budgets and persisted history. - Closure path: User request → contextual observation → model step/action choice → constrained tool execution → new screenshot/DOM/tool result and notes → subsequent model step or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The documented local Web/Desktop CLI and remote task modes instantiate this actor loop using bundled AgentKit specs and first-party tool dispatch; no test-only harness or adjacent research agent is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive environment actions after seeing observations. Tool schemas, transport, budget and safety gates constrain or carry this discretion rather than select it. - Evidence: Web agent specification, Desktop step executor, runtime architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the first-party model/tool loop; no live hosted-provider success claim. - Caveats: S1 depends on configured model/device/backend availability, and the assessment does not measure task efficacy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate interference between distinct simultaneously admitted batch task attempts, especially duplicate execution, resource oversubscription and accidental state reuse. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent attempts may claim the same task, worker/device capacity or prepared environment after restarts, timeouts and parallel preparation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select or revise which attempt can claim a lease and when backpressured capacity may admit subsequent tasks; a higher-level autonomous coordination policy would have to be composed to own the discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic scheduler, leases and persisted rules select immediate admission based on human/developer-provided limits; there is no demonstrated autonomous agent choosing inter-task coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite task claim/heartbeat, attempt identity, task-scoped sandbox/device lease, backpressure samples, single-use prepared reservation and reconciliation. - Closure path: Contending batch attempts → exclusive task/lease claim and controller-pressure check → one attempt proceeds, conflicting contenders wait or reconcile → subsequent tasks claim only released/eligible capacity. - Boundary reachability: Batch mode ships these scheduler/lease components and documented resume semantics; unlike a hypothetical generic queue, the code specifically handles simultaneous task claims and attempt reuse. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete inter-S1 conflict attenuation exists, but its decisive runtime rule is deterministic; a first-party S2-specific constructor path is exposed without a shipped model-owned policy adjustment loop. - Distinct S1 units: Independently running Desktop/Web task attempts with their own task goals, environments and actor loops under a single batch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Duplicate task submissions or competing sandbox/device admissions and reused application state when attempts overlap or a runner resumes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Unique task/attempt claims and task-scoped leases, bounded concurrency, backpressure gating and selected retry/reconcile rules. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Denied/late claims do not start duplicate actor operations; capacity availability and persisted task status determine later submission and resume. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The coordination enforces a named cross-attempt collision and resource contention invariant and feeds a concrete admission/withholding result into subsequent distinct task attempts. - Evidence: batch scheduler, lease coordinator, backpressure gate, batch recovery documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for an actual inter-attempt coordination path, medium for constructor classification. - Caveats: The batch is optional; an individual local run has no distinct S2 peer set. Deterministic conflict avoidance cannot be upgraded to S2=A without autonomous coordination discretion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Whole-batch current commitments and running capacity can be supervised by a legitimate operator who controls admission, pause, cancellation, drain and selected retries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed or uncertain attempts, exhausted retry budgets, workload pressure, controller interruptions and cleanup problems across a batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: View current aggregate task statuses and choose to stop new admissions, cancel current work, drain running tasks, resume or authorize specific failed-task retries. - Decision owner: The first-party mode&#39;s human batch operator. The scheduler and SQLite state apply that returned decision; no independent AI whole-batch manager is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status/report CLI, persisted control flags and task states, pause/drain/cancel handlers, selected-retry plan validation and scheduler poller. - Closure path: Operator reviews whole-batch status/report → invokes pause/drain/cancel or bounded selected retry/resume → persistent flags/attempt ceilings are read by the live scheduler → current/new task admissions and execution change. - Boundary reachability: The documented northcua batch status|report|pause|drain|cancel|resume commands and scheduler&#39;s control-flag poller form a usable first-party operator mode over the same persisted batch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is an operationally returned parent mode (P), not automatic agent S3; deterministic capacity thresholds and external operator actions do not supply autonomous S3=A or C by themselves. - Whole-system current view: Persisted all-task states and report/failure breakdown across one identified batch, including attempts and evaluation statuses. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume new admissions, cancel active work, drain committed tasks and select permitted retries for failed tasks, affecting whole-batch present commitments. - Evidence: batch operator commands, CLI branch handling, control-flag scheduler, selected retry plan. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; owner/control return is directly coded, but no actual operator run was reproduced in this assessment. - Caveats: Parent governance is only at a single batch&#39;s current-operations recursion, not at the OSS project&#39;s policy/identity recursion. This finding does not classify arbitrary status dashboards as S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A separate evaluator agent challenges actor completion against independently gathered artifact and optionally live application reality. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent self-report may disagree with task requirements, unsaved application state, missing artifacts or unseen outcome failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select relevant raw/recorded evidence and optional live probes, judge requirements and submit a structured success/failure report distinct from actor termination. - Decision owner: The separately instantiated first-party model-driven evaluator, through its own tool-selection and final-report loop; not the actor, trace writer or deterministic batch status projector. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evidence manifest, read-only artifact and live probe tools, bounded evaluator step execution, report schema, fail-open/fail-closed configuration and batch result projection. - Closure path: Actor completion → independent artifact/live evidence collection → evaluator model verdict in finalreport → recorded outcome distinct from actor claim → batch task becomes success/failure/pending and controls downstream retry/reporting decisions. - Boundary reachability: Shipped local/remote Web/Desktop evaluator modes can be enabled in app configuration or CLI, and the Web evaluator runs before releasing the actor&#39;s original browser session. A direct live probe is conditional on explicit permission; the artifact-evidence route remains independently wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evaluator chooses direct evidence inspection and a task-grounded judgment in its own model/tool loop; its report changes terminal status. Deterministic tooling does not make that independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: Whether the actor actually satisfied the user&#39;s task despite its own reported completion. - Ordinary reporting path: Actor terminal result, its model/tool traces, screenshots and action outputs. - Complementary access path: Separate evaluator&#39;s evidence selection and tool access to saved artifacts plus, when enabled, fresh DOM/screenshot or allowed device read-only probes against the original live session. - Independence boundary: The evaluator executes in a separate model-round/runtime and has evidence/probe tools unavailable to ordinary self-report; it is still a first-party evaluation path, not an externally independent scientific audit or proof of blind reviewer independence. - Who acts on findings: Batch status projection treats a present negative verdict as failure, while operator/retry flow may admit a subsequent attempt; no claim that the same actor is automatically corrected before finalization. - Evidence: evaluator loop, Web evaluator service, read-only probe contracts, terminal projection, evaluation modes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for independently wired model audit and returned verdict, not for real-world judge accuracy. - Caveats: Evaluation may be disabled or fail open; live probes require permission and original session availability; a status verdict affects subsequent batch handling rather than guaranteeing a corrective rerun. No actual hosted model, external evaluator or empirical accuracy study was executed for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped S4-specific prospective external-environment model and adaptation-option decision returning to present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in apps/sites, task complexity and model quality are seen by current actor/evaluator, but not separately explored as future environment adaptation at the batch/harness recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generate future-facing capability/policy adaptation options from external distinctions and negotiate them with current operational control. - Decision owner: Project developers/operators may separately plan upgrades; actor plans current tasks, evaluator judges completed work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Working notes, image/context compaction, feedback artifacts, model/provider configuration, benchmark and research documentation. - Closure path: Task observations and evaluator reports feed current run/batch status, not a first-party outside-and-then option/implementation return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task planning, context memory, runtime retries and post-run quality reporting are not themselves prospective adaptation judgments. - Evidence: system tour, context mechanisms, batch lifecycle. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Research and model upgrades can support future work outside this runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No system-level identity or ultimate-policy resolution channel with a legitimate deciding owner and returned binding decision is shipped for the assessed task/batch organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky computer actions, provider/device access, evaluation-probe mutability and cost/time limits are bounded by operator-set configuration and tool controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Adjudicate contested identity/purpose/ultimate-policy matters and return binding changed policy to the running organization. - Decision owner: Users/developers choose static configuration and external provider access; no first-party S5 decision actor or parent appeal path is established for such matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only evaluator probes by default, explicit mutating-tool opt-in, sandbox capabilities, model/task budgets, run authorization and cancellation. - Closure path: Current operator configuration → enforcement of permitted tools/budgets, with no identity-level proposal → parent authority → decision → changed operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordinary permission and task cancellation choices are not ultimate-policy decisions, and no shipped independent policy/identity deliberation return is identified. - Evidence: evaluation configuration, batch configuration, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at assessed operation/batch boundary. - Caveats: Project maintainers naturally retain licensing/release governance, but that adjacent project-level authority cannot be silently credited as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557/assessments/no/northcua.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557\">0ad99b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:13:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">halay</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay/tree/49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\">49bccfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct user-task agents operate in their assigned project directories through the launched configured CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requests, project state and subprocess/tool feedback delivered to each CLI session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each running model-backed CLI agent chooses task actions and next turns, while halay controls the first-party spawning, routing, replies and child communication interface. - Decision owner: Individual CLI-backed model task actor, through halay&#39;s installed multi-agent composition; not Node HTTP request routing alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backends, per-agent cwd/account/model, MCP swarm tools, event stream, permissions and optional worktrees. - Closure path: User/conductor task→halay launches addressed CLI agent→upstream engine acts in workspace→result/status returns via halay→model continues, receives follow-up or returns result. - Boundary reachability: src/server.js launches supported CLI processes and src/swarm-mcp.js exposes actionable tools; this requires one external CLI installed and authenticated, and does not credit its underlying internal tool loop as a first-party implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Real CLI-backed models have discretion over work; the first-party control plane enables and observes their running roles. The launcher alone would not be autonomous. - Evidence: spawn and task lifecycle, backends, swarm tools. - Basis: Supported executable agent-CLI composition and returned operational state, not claimed first-party model internals. - Confidence: Medium-high for first-party runtime integration, not upstream vendor correctness. - Caveats: The prerequisite external CLI supplies cognition, local tool semantics and vendor behavior; absent a configured backend, halay is an idle control plane.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately demonstrated autonomous attenuation of an observed conflict among the multiple operating child units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shared-workspace collisions and competing task dependencies are plausible; the default task DAG orders work but is authored and assigned as a workflow, not measured inter-S1 contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party dependency gate rejects blocked starts; conductor can manually/model-select routing as S3 rather than independent S2 balancing. - Decision owner: User/conductor for assignments, deterministic task dependency guard for eligibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task graph dependson, worktrees, agent message bus, parent-child scoped tools and queues. - Closure path: Task dependency→ready/waiting/blocked gate or manual assignment; messages travel between actors, but no installed contention sensing→S2 arbitration→revised peer behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool messages, worktree isolation and static dependency checks are valuable coordination infrastructure without evidence of a specific separately owned S2 regulator. - Evidence: task dependency/agent management, swarm tool interface. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing strongest task-DAG and peer-messaging candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A conductor can respond to a user-observed conflict, but no distinct routine S2 feedback mandate was found in the shipped setup.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Shipped model-driven conductor supervises the present agent fleet and allocates/revises task commitments and capacity. - Whole-system current view: Conductor&#39;s listagents, task listing, status/attention, child-result notifications and account availability expose the bounded installed fleet and current work backlog, rather than a single worker&#39;s perspective. - Current-control decision scope: Conductor can choose and revise work-to-child allocation, active agent count/model/folder, follow-up task instructions and interruption in response to observed fleet state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent user requests across projects, idle/busy/blocked children, failed tasks, approval pauses, delayed results and current model/account capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor chooses whether to create or reuse an agent, what folder/model/account to assign, when to message or interrupt it and how to react to its returned result/status. - Decision owner: Conductor model running with the bundled role prompt and privileged first-party swarm MCP rights; runtime enforces access scopes and completion notifications. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents, startagent, sendmessage, readagent, task assignment, stop/interrupt, child-finish notifications, persistent fleet state and task dependency gates. - Closure path: User&#39;s multi-project goal→conductor lists fleet/task state→selects agent and assignment→agent acts→halay notifies its completion/error/block→conductor revises instructions, assignments or informs user. - Boundary reachability: A shipped Swarm chat has its own conductor role and tool permissions; unlike a passive dashboard it can model-select concrete task allocation from returned runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party conductor prompt gives the model an operational portfolio mandate and action rights; subprocess state and database enforcement support rather than replace its choices. - Evidence: conductor mandate, scoped MCP actions, fleet/task state and wakeup, notification format. - Basis: Reachable first-party conductor perception→discretionary allocation/revision→child-action→status feedback over a bounded fleet. - Confidence: High for installed mandate and capability, not measured real fleet performance. - Caveats: The conductor&#39;s decision model runs via a required external CLI and may fail to act on feedback; human controls, context budgets and per-child permissions constrain it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent alternate evidence path produces an audit verdict returned to conductor beyond ordinary agent and tool reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False child completion claims and unsafe changes could be reviewed by humans; no separate installed auditing reviewer independently samples operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor can read child transcript, summaries and git status, but this is ordinary supervision; approvals are chosen by the human. - Decision owner: Conductor for monitoring and operator for approvals; no complementary auditor with independent judgment/return authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent transcript, worktree state, browser UI, status notifications, reviewable git changes and authentication. - Closure path: Child events→conductor/UI; no independent artifact sampling/replay→adversarial audit finding→corrective S3 loop in standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate transcript reader does not gain a distinct independent observational channel, and the human may optionally review but is outside first-party installed audit. - Evidence: swarm read/status, event handling. - Basis: Bounded absence review of strongest transcript/worktree evidence paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External CLI vendors or human code review may supply external auditors, not credited to halay by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuous prospective environment analysis selects future capability/organizational changes for this fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New CLI backends, future user work, schedule timing, model prices and capacity changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures accounts/backends and schedules; conductor handles current task choices and user-described future jobs. - Decision owner: Human operator for product/organizational evolution, conductor model for present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backend discovery, scheduled/recurring tasks, durable bots, peer configuration and model selection. - Closure path: User configuration/schedule→current or timed execution; no outside environmental forecast→strategic option selection→negotiation with current S3 and adopted future operating rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scheduled execution and selectable backends are operational capacity, not autonomous anticipation of changing external circumstances. - Evidence: server and task schedule, conductor instructions, bots. - Basis: Bounded absence review including strongest persistent/scheduled mode. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Conductor can be asked to research future changes as a one-off S1 work item; no permanent S4 function follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct legitimate ultimate identity-policy role owns and closes an organizational charter/mandate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Privileged agent management, resource/cost limits, malicious agents, user identity/authentication and permission escalation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authenticated human owner sets controls; static role access enforces conductor versus child scope, and the user decides tool approvals. - Decision owner: Operator for legitimate ultimate constraints, deterministic auth/MCP guard for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Passkeys, per-agent bearer tokens, parent ownership checks, approval mode, model/account boundaries and persistent configuration. - Closure path: Operator setting or approval→guarded API behavior; no autonomous or parent-assisted identity-policy deliberation and returned authoritative mandate to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong security and an exclusive conductor role are not sufficient for an independent governance legitimacy cycle. - Evidence: auth/access, MCP scoping, server. - Basis: Bounded absence after screening legitimate operator policy and role privilege paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A human-led institution could govern an installed halay fleet; it is not a supplied self-governing S5 actor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014/assessments/ha/halay.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014\">0d074c2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
